^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,0)="8.0^3051119^3020906^1"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,0)="^^675^675^3051119"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,1,0)="MailMan 8.0"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,2,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,3,0)="Test Sites: FORUM, San Francisco, Central Alabama, FO-Albany, FO-Oakland"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,4,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,5,0)="MailMan 8.0 is a maintenance release.  It includes the complete MailMan"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,6,0)="suite of data dictionaries, but it does not populate the files.  This"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,7,0)="version expects that a site already has MailMan 7.1 installed and running,"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,8,0)="and that it is patched through XM*7.1*198.  It is also expected that"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,9,0)="Kernel 8.0 and FileMan 22.0 are installed and are reasonably up-to-date"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,10,0)="(through patches XU*8.0*216 and DI*22.0*68, respectively)."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,11,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,12,0)="MailMan 8.0 offers the following improvements over MailMan 7.1:"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,13,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,14,0)="1. MailMan is now DNS aware.  It can use the Kernel API MAIL^XLFNSLK"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,15,0)="to retrieve IP addresses.  It is no longer necessary to manually update the"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,16,0)="IP addresses in the DOMAIN file, 4.2.  The IP address fields will remain in"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,17,0)="file 4.2, and MailMan will use them.  However, if they don't work, MailMan"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,18,0)="will use the Kernel API to retrieve a list of valid IP addresses.  When"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,19,0)="MailMan finds one that works, MailMan will replace the non-working IP"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,20,0)="address with the working one.  In order to activate DNS awareness, this"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,21,0)="new field in the MAILMAN SITE PARAMETERS file 4.3 must be set to 'yes':"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,22,0)="     4.3,8.22   DNS AWARE                        Yes or No?"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,23,0)="Also, routine ^XLFNSLK must exist, and the following field in the"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,24,0)="KERNEL SYSTEMS PARAMETERS file 8989.3 must be properly filled in with"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,25,0)="an IP address:"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,26,0)="     8989.3,51  DNS IP                           IP address for DNS"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,27,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,28,0)="2. For TCP/IP connections, MailMan can now build transmission scripts"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,29,0)="on-the-fly.  (Transmission scripts exist in the DOMAIN file 4.2, in the"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,30,0)="TRANSMISSION SCRIPT multiple, in the TEXT field 2.)  If the TYPE is ""SMTP"","
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,31,0)="""TCPCHAN"", or null, and the TEXT field has no records, and these fields"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,32,0)="in the MAILMAN SITE PARAMETERS file 4.3 are filled in:"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,33,0)="     4.3,8.23   TCP/IP COMMUNICATIONS PROTOCOL   Points to file 3.4"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,34,0)="     4.3,8.24   TCP/IP TRANSMISSION SCRIPT       Points to file 4.6"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,35,0)="...then MailMan creates the script.  This one is for FORUM:"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,36,0)="     O H=DOMAIN.EXT,P=TCP/IP-MAILMAN"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,37,0)="     C TCPCHAN-SOCKET25"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,38,0)="where:"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,39,0)="     ""DOMAIN.EXT"" is taken from the DOMAIN name."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,40,0)="     ""TCP/IP-MAILMAN"" is the protocol pointed to by field 8.23"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,41,0)="     ""TCPCHAN-SOCKET25"" is the script pointed to by field 8.24."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,42,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,43,0)="3. Messages in transmit queues can now be designated as low priority, as"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,44,0)="well as high priority.  If a message gets stuck in a transmit queue and is"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,45,0)="holding up the rest of the queue for whatever reason, MailMan will make"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,46,0)="that message a low priority message, so that all the other messages are"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,47,0)="transmitted ahead of it.  The postmaster can also make these priority"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,48,0)="changes.  In the message queue, high-priority messages are now marked with"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,49,0)="'^', instead of '$'.  Low priority messages are marked with 'v'."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,50,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,51,0)="The postmaster can now change the transmit priority at the message level"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,52,0)="(at the 'Message action: Ignore//' prompt).  As at the basket level, the"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,53,0)="command to use is 'X'.  (In a user basket, the 'X' at the message level is"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,54,0)="a command to unload a PackMan message or KIDS build.  In a remote transmit"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,55,0)="queue, the 'X' changes the transmit priority.  The difference is the"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,56,0)="context, and writers of MailMan front-ends should take note!)"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,57,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,58,0)="4. MailMan date/times are now in a standard format, produced by the Kernel"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,59,0)="API: $$FMTE^XLFDT(datetime,""2Z"").  Previously, 3020803.153204 would be"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,60,0)="displayed as '03 Aug 02 15:32'.  Now, it is displayed as '08/03/02@15:32'."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,61,0)="This change is also carried through to all MailMan APIs which return"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,62,0)="date/time in MailMan format.  Users of such APIs should take note!"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,63,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,64,0)="5. MailMan remote message IDs now include the message date, to ensure that"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,65,0)="if you are told that a message is a duplicate of a previously received"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,66,0)="message, it really is.  Sites will no longer have problems sending messages"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,67,0)="from a production account to a test account which was created by ""mirroring"""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,68,0)="the production account.  The remote message ID is now the message number"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,69,0)="following by a period, followed by the 7-digit FileMan message creation"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,70,0)="date.  Before, a remote message ID might be 34561234@DOMAIN.EXT.  Now it"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,71,0)="would be 34561234.3020803@DOMAIN.EXT."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,72,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,73,0)="6. The ^XMC*, ^XMR*, ^XMS* suite of routines, which are responsible for"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,74,0)="scheduling, transmitting to, and receiving messages from remote sites, have"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,75,0)="been completely overhauled to make them easier to understand and easier to"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,76,0)="maintain."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,77,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,78,0)="7. MailMan will no longer display user names by taking them directly from"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,79,0)="the .01 field of the NEW PERSON file, 200.  The API, $$NAMEFMT^XLFNAME,"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,80,0)="supplied as part of the Name Standardization project, is used, instead."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,81,0)="Thus, the names of people whose last names, for instance, contain periods,"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,82,0)="apostrophes, or spaces, are properly displayed (ST. IVES, O'MALLEY, and"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,83,0)="VAN DYKE), instead of improperly (STIVES, OMALLEY, and VANDYKE)."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,84,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,85,0)="8. In response to NOIS FAV-0202-71819, messages with responses may no longer"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,86,0)="be forwarded to broadcast to all users.  Such messages may have important"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,87,0)="information in the responses, and as we all know, responses are not auto-"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,88,0)="forwarded to remote sites for users with auto-forward addresses.  Users who"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,89,0)="attempt to broadcast messages with responses will be encouraged to copy the"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,90,0)="message and its responses into a new message, which can be broadcast."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,91,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,92,0)="9. Incoming PackMan and KIDS messages are no longer subject to the"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,93,0)="restriction NETWORK - MAX LINES RECEIVE (field 8.31, file 4.3).  Other kinds"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,94,0)="of messages continue to be subject to that restriction."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,95,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,96,0)="10. If a task transmitting messages to another site fails and has to be"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,97,0)="requeued, it really is requeued.  Previously, that wasn't true.  Previously,"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,98,0)="the failing task queued up a new task to take its place, and then the"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,99,0)="failing task stopped."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,100,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,101,0)="11. You can now send, forward, or filter-forward messages to H.device"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,102,0)="(Headerless print).  It will print the message, without a header, on the"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,103,0)="device.  As before, sending messages to D.device will print them with"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,104,0)="a header."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,105,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,106,0)="12. In response to NOIS HWH-0502-41774, if you become a surrogate of a user,"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,107,0)="and try to access a message that the user is not on (but you are), you will"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,108,0)="be told that you will have to forward the message to the user, in order to"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,109,0)="access it as the user.  You will then be given the opportunity to forward"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,110,0)="it, if it's OK to do so.  (For example, if the message is closed, you would"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,111,0)="not be allowed to forward it, if you didn't send it.)"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,112,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,113,0)="13. In response to NOIS CHY-0597-50209, when using TalkMan to communicate"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,114,0)="with a site, MailMan will only consider using scripts whose TYPE is TELNET."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,115,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,116,0)="14. The pre-init, PRE^XMYPRE, will"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,117,0)="- K ^DOPT(""XMC""), to reset the programmer options (D ^XMC)."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,118,0)="- Kill the old ""AC"" xref definition for NETWORK PRIORITY TRANSMISSION field"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,119,0)="6, in the basket multiple of file 3.7, and change all xrefs"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,120,0)="^XMB(3.7,.5,2,""AC"",1,XMK,XMZ) to ^XMB(3.7,.5,2,XMK,1,""AC"",1,XMZ)."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,121,0)="- Delete the following fields from file 4.2999, MESSAGE STATISTICS, because"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,122,0)="they are not used:"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,123,0)="     7  OUTGOING MESSAGE COUNT"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,124,0)="     8  CHARACTERS REC'D"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,125,0)="     9  INCOMING MESSAGE COUNT"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,126,0)="    10  CHARACTERS SENT "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,127,0)="- Delete the ENTRY ACTION fields for options XMQSHOW, XMSUBEDIT, XMDXPROT."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,128,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,129,0)="15. The post-init, POST^XMYPRE, will"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,130,0)="- Go through MailMan's security keys and ensure that the 'mutually"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,131,0)="exclusive' keys are correct."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,132,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,133,0)="16. Here are DD changes:"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,134,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,135,0)="a. MESSAGE file 3.7"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,136,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,137,0)="- Change field 2, FORWARDING ADDRESS to fix the bulletin xref.  FileMan"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,138,0)="thought that it was firing XM-FWD-ADDRESS-CHECKER, yet it was actually"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,139,0)="firing XMZFWDBULL.  It now fires XM FWD ADDRESS CHANGE, a new bulletin,"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,140,0)="which looks like the old ones, but has a better name."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,141,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,142,0)="STANDARD DATA DICTIONARY #3.7 -- MAILBOX FILE"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,143,0)="STORED IN ^XMB(3.7,"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,144,0)="DATA        NAME                GLOBAL        DATA"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,145,0)="ELEMENT     TITLE               LOCATION      TYPE"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,146,0)="---------------------------------------------------------------------------"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,147,0)="3.7,2     FORWARDING ADDRESS     0;2 FREE TEXT"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,148,0)="          Remote address to which mail is routed   "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,149,0)="          INPUT TRANSFORM:  D XMFWD^XMVVITA(.X,$G(XMIA)) I $D(X) K:$L(X)>50"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,150,0)="                            !($L(X)<1) X"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,151,0)="          LAST EDITED:      APR 23, 2002 "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,152,0)="          HELP-PROMPT:      Enter a forwarding address (name@domain) of up "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,153,0)="                            to 50 characters. "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,154,0)="          DESCRIPTION:      This field names an additional address to which"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,155,0)="                            any messages addressed to you are routed.  This"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,156,0)="                            address must be a remote address or a local"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,157,0)="                            device.  "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,158,0)="                                 "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,159,0)="                            If you want a local user to see your messages,"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,160,0)="                            make that user a surrogate.  "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,161,0)="                                 "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,162,0)="                            See the LOCAL DELIVERY FLAG.  If you do not"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,163,0)="                            edit this field to 'ON', then mail will only be"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,164,0)="                            delivered to your FORWARDING ADDRESS and not to"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,165,0)="                            your Mailbox.  "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,166,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,167,0)="          NOTES:            XXXX--CAN'T BE ALTERED EXCEPT BY PROGRAMMER"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,168,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,169,0)="          CROSS-REFERENCE:  ^^BULLETIN MESSAGE "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,170,0)="                            1)= S Y(0)=X,D0=DA X ""N DIIND F DIIND=1.001:.00"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,171,0)="                            1 Q:$D(^DD(3.7,2,1,1,DIIND))[0  X ^(DIIND)"" K X"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,172,0)="                            MY S XMB=""XM FWD ADDRESS CHANGE"" D ^XMB:$D(^XMB"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,173,0)="                            (3.7,""B"",XMB)),ENT^XMUT7(DA) K Y,XMB"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,174,0)="                            1.001)= S X=Y(0) S Y(1)=$S($D(^XMB(3.7,D0,0)):^"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,175,0)="                            (0),1:"""") S X=$S('$D(^VA(200,+$P(Y(1),U,1),0)):"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,176,0)="                            """",1:$P(^(0),U,1)) S XMB(1)=X"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,177,0)="                            1.002)= S X=Y(0) S Y(1)=$S($D(^XMB(3.7,D0,0)):^"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,178,0)="                            (0),1:"""") S X=$P(Y(1),U,2) S XMB(2)=X"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,179,0)="                            2)= Q"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,180,0)="                            CREATE PARAMETER #1)= NAME"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,181,0)="                            CREATE PARAMETER #2)= FORWARDING ADDRESS"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,182,0)="                            CREATE VALUE)= XM FWD ADDRESS CHANGE"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,183,0)="                            DELETE VALUE)= NO EFFECT"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,184,0)="                            This cross reference sends two bulletins.  "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,185,0)="                                 "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,186,0)="                            The first, XM FWD ADDRESS CHANGE, notifies"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,187,0)="                            responsible individuals (belonging to the mail"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,188,0)="                            group associated with the bulletin) that a user "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,189,0)="                            has changed his forwarding address.  This is"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,190,0)="                            the ""official"" bulletin of this cross"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,191,0)="                            reference.  "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,192,0)="                                 "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,193,0)="                            The second, XM FWD ADDRESS CHECK, is sent to"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,194,0)="                            the user's new forwarding address as test to"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,195,0)="                            see if the address is valid.  If it's not"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,196,0)="                            valid, the sender (the Postmaster) will receive"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,197,0)="                            an error message.  This bulletin has been"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,198,0)="                            piggy-backed onto the ""official"" bulletin. "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,199,0)="                            FileMan is not aware of this.  "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,200,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,201,0)="          CROSS-REFERENCE:  3.7^F "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,202,0)="                            1)= S ^XMB(3.7,""F"",$E(X,1,30),DA)="""""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,203,0)="                            2)= K ^XMB(3.7,""F"",$E(X,1,30),DA)"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,204,0)="                            This cross reference enables the site manager"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,205,0)="                            to easily find a user who enters a bad"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,206,0)="                            forwarding address.  "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,207,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,208,0)="- Change field 3.702,6 - NETWORK PRIORITY TRANSMISSION"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,209,0)="to expand the set of codes to add low priority, and to change the ""AC"" xref"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,210,0)="from user-level to message-level.  (See item 3, above, for more info.)"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,211,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,212,0)="3.702,6     NETWORK PRIORITY TRANSMISSION? 0;6 SET"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,213,0)="                            '1' FOR HIGH PRIORITY; "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,214,0)="                            '0' FOR REGULAR PRIORITY; "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,215,0)="                            '2' FOR LOW PRIORITY; "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,216,0)="            HELP-PROMPT:    How should this message be handled during "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,217,0)="                            transmission? "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,218,0)="            DESCRIPTION:    This field is used to flag messages that need"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,219,0)="                            to be transmitted before or after all other"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,220,0)="                            messages in a queue.  "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,221,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,222,0)="                            Messages in the queue which are flagged as HIGH"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,223,0)="                            priority are transmitted before all other"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,224,0)="                            messages.  "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,225,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,226,0)="                            Messages in the queue which are flagged as"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,227,0)="                            REGULAR priority (default) are transmitted"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,228,0)="                            next.  "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,229,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,230,0)="                            Messages in the queue which are flagged as LOW"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,231,0)="                            priority are transmitted after all other"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,232,0)="                            messages.  "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,233,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,234,0)="                            Messages with the same priority are transmitted"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,235,0)="                            in IEN order, which is not necessarily the"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,236,0)="                            order in which they appear in the transmit"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,237,0)="                            queue.  "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,238,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,239,0)="                            Only the POSTMASTER (or surrogate) may change"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,240,0)="                            the priority of a message in a transmit queue. "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,241,0)="                            If, during a transmission, MailMan has problems "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,242,0)="                            with a particular message, MailMan may flag"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,243,0)="                            that message as LOW priority, so that other"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,244,0)="                            messages in the queue will transmit, before the"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,245,0)="                            problem message is attempted again.  "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,246,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,247,0)="                            Changes to message priorities can be made even"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,248,0)="                            while a queue is being transmitted.  "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,249,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,250,0)="            FIELD INDEX:    AC (#12)    REGULAR    IR    SORTING ONLY"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,251,0)="              Short Descr:  Transmission Priority Cross Reference"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,252,0)="              Description:  This xref is used by MailMan during message"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,253,0)="                            transmission to identify messages which should"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,254,0)="                            be transmitted first (high priority) or last "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,255,0)="                            (low priority).  Messages with regular priority"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,256,0)="                            are not xref'd."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,257,0)="                Set Logic:  S ^XMB(3.7,DA(2),2,DA(1),1,""AC"",$E(X,1,1),DA)="""""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,258,0)="                 Set Cond:  S X=X"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,259,0)="               Kill Logic:  K ^XMB(3.7,DA(2),2,DA(1),1,""AC"",$E(X,1,1),DA)"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,260,0)="                Kill Cond:  S X=X"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,261,0)="               Whole Kill:  K ^XMB(3.7,DA(2),2,DA(1),1,""AC"")"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,262,0)="                     X(1):  NETWORK PRIORITY TRANSMISSION?  (3.702,6)"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,263,0)="                            (Subscr 1)  (Len 1)  (forwards)"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,264,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,265,0)="b. MAIL GROUP file 3.8, add a trigger to field .01 to populate field 5.1"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,266,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,267,0)="STANDARD DATA DICTIONARY #3.8 -- MAIL GROUP FILE"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,268,0)="STORED IN ^XMB(3.8,"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,269,0)="DATA       NAME                  GLOBAL        DATA"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,270,0)="ELEMENT    TITLE                 LOCATION      TYPE"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,271,0)="----------------------------------------------------------------------------"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,272,0)="3.8,.01    NAME                   0;1 FREE TEXT (Required)"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,273,0)="           MAIL GROUP NAME           "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,274,0)="           INPUT TRANSFORM:  S:X?1""G."".E!(X?1""g."".E) X=$E(X,3,99) K:$L(X)>30"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,275,0)="                             !($L(X)<3)!'(X'?1P.E)!(X'?.ANP) X"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,276,0)="           HELP-PROMPT:      ANSWER MUST BE 3-30 CHARACTERS IN LENGTH "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,277,0)="           DESCRIPTION:      The name of a mail group, i.e. a list of"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,278,0)="                             recipients who can all be addressed at once by"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,279,0)="                             reference to this name.  "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,280,0)="           NOTES:            XXXX--CAN'T BE ALTERED EXCEPT BY PROGRAMMER"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,281,0)="           CROSS-REFERENCE:  3.8^B "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,282,0)="                             1)= S ^XMB(3.8,""B"",$E(X,1,30),DA)="""""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,283,0)="                             2)= K ^XMB(3.8,""B"",$E(X,1,30),DA)"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,284,0)="           CROSS-REFERENCE:  ^^TRIGGER^3.8^5 "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,285,0)="                             1)= X ^DD(3.8,.01,1,2,1.3) I X S X=DIV S Y(1)=$"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,286,0)="                             S($D(^XMB(3.8,D0,3)):^(3),1:"""") S X=$P(Y(1),U,1"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,287,0)="                             ),X=X S DIU=X K Y S X=DIV S X=$G(XMDUZ,DUZ) S D"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,288,0)="                             IH=$G(^XMB(3.8,DIV(0),3)),DIV=X S $P(^(3),U,1)="
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,289,0)="                             DIV,DIH=3.8,DIG=5 D ^DICR"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,290,0)="                             1.3)= K DIV S DIV=X,D0=DA,DIV(0)=D0 S Y(0)=X S "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,291,0)="                             Y(1)=$S($D(^XMB(3.8,D0,3)):^(3),1:"""") S X=$S('$"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,292,0)="                             D(^VA(200,+$P(Y(1),U,1),0)):"""",1:$P(^(0),U,1))="
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,293,0)="                             """""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,294,0)="                             2)= Q"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,295,0)="                             CREATE CONDITION)= ORGANIZER="""""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,296,0)="                             CREATE VALUE)= S X=$G(XMDUZ,DUZ)"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,297,0)="                             DELETE VALUE)= NO EFFECT"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,298,0)="                             FIELD)= ORGANIZER"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,299,0)="                             When a user creates a mail group, this trigger"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,300,0)="                             makes that user the organizer of the group. "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,301,0)="                             The user is free to change that field.  "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,302,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,303,0)="c. INTER-UCI TRANSFER file 4.281, increase domain name field lengths to "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,304,0)="agree with .01 field in file 4.2.  Also increase lengths of B & C xrefs."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,305,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,306,0)="STANDARD DATA DICTIONARY #4.281 -- INTER-UCI TRANSFER FILE"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,307,0)="STORED IN ^%ZISL(4.281,"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,308,0)="DATA        NAME                GLOBAL        DATA"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,309,0)="ELEMENT     TITLE               LOCATION      TYPE"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,310,0)="---------------------------------------------------------------------------"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,311,0)="4.281,.01   FROM DOMAIN            0;1 FREE TEXT (Required)"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,312,0)="            INPUT TRANSFORM:  K:$L(X)>64!($L(X)<3)!'(X'?1P.E) X"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,313,0)="            HELP-PROMPT:      Answer must be 3-64 characters in length"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,314,0)="            CROSS-REFERENCE:  4.281^B"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,315,0)="                              1)= S ^%ZISL(4.281,""B"",$E(X,1,64),DA)="""""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,316,0)="                              2)= K ^%ZISL(4.281,""B"",$E(X,1,64),DA)"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,317,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,318,0)="4.281,1     TO DOMAIN              0;2 FREE TEXT"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,319,0)="            INPUT TRANSFORM:  K:$L(X)>64!($L(X)<3) X"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,320,0)="            HELP-PROMPT:      Answer must be 3-64 characters in length"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,321,0)="            CROSS-REFERENCE:  4.281^C"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,322,0)="                              1)= S ^%ZISL(4.281,""C"",$E(X,1,64),DA)="""""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,323,0)="                              2)= K ^%ZISL(4.281,""C"",$E(X,1,64),DA)"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,324,0)="                              3)= XXXX - Do not delete"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,325,0)="                              This cross-reference is used by the receiving"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,326,0)="                              UCI to find entries which have been"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,327,0)="                              'transmitted' to it from other UCIs."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,328,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,329,0)="d. Add fields to MESSAGE STATISTICS file 4.2999."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,330,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,331,0)="STANDARD DATA DICTIONARY #4.2999 -- MESSAGE STATISTICS FILE"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,332,0)="STORED IN ^XMBS(4.2999,"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,333,0)="DATA        NAME                  GLOBAL        DATA"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,334,0)="ELEMENT     TITLE                 LOCATION      TYPE"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,335,0)="---------------------------------------------------------------------------"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,336,0)="4.2999,8    DIRECTION              3;8 SET"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,337,0)="                              'S' FOR SENDING; "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,338,0)="                              'R' FOR RECEIVING; "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,339,0)="            HELP-PROMPT:      Are we sending or receiving? "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,340,0)="            DESCRIPTION:      This field is used to report whether, at any"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,341,0)="                              given time, we are sending messages to, or"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,342,0)="                              receiving messages from, the site.  "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,343,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,344,0)="4.2999,48   XMIT IP ADDRESSES TRIED 4;8 FREE TEXT"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,345,0)="            INPUT TRANSFORM:  K:$L(X)>100!($L(X)<3) X"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,346,0)="            HELP-PROMPT:      Answer must be 3-100 characters in length."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,347,0)="            DESCRIPTION:    This is a list of IP addresses, separated by"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,348,0)="                            commas, which have already been used in"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,349,0)="                            attempting to connect with the site."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,350,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,351,0)="                            We use this list in conjunction with the DNS"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,352,0)="                            server, which returns a list of valid addresses"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,353,0)="                            to try.  Using both lists, we can come up with"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,354,0)="                            the next IP address to try."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,355,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,356,0)="4.29992,3   XMIT AUDIT IP ADDRESS  0;4 FREE TEXT"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,357,0)="            INPUT TRANSFORM:  K:$L(X)>20!($L(X)<1) X"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,358,0)="            HELP-PROMPT:      Answer must be 1-20 characters in length. "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,359,0)="            DESCRIPTION:      This is the IP address used for this attempt."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,360,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,361,0)="e. Change fields in MESSAGE STATISTICS file 4.2999."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,362,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,363,0)="Changed the title and description:"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,364,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,365,0)="4.2999,.01  NAME              0;1 POINTER TO DOMAIN FILE (#4.2)"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,366,0)="                                 (Required)"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,367,0)="            Domain name               "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,368,0)="            INPUT TRANSFORM:  S:$D(X) DINUM=X"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,369,0)="            DESCRIPTION:      Domain for which network mail transmission"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,370,0)="                              information is collected."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,371,0)="            NOTES:            XXXX--CAN'T BE ALTERED EXCEPT BY PROGRAMMER"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,372,0)="            CROSS-REFERENCE:  4.2999^B"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,373,0)="                              1)= S ^XMBS(4.2999,""B"",$E(X,1,30),DA)="""""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,374,0)="                              2)= K ^XMBS(4.2999,""B"",$E(X,1,30),DA)"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,375,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,376,0)="Changed to a pointer and changed the help prompt:"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,377,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,378,0)="4.2999,2    MESSAGE IN TRANSIT     3;2 POINTER TO MESSAGE FILE (#3.9)"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,379,0)="            HELP-PROMPT:    Enter the IEN of the message being transmitted."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,380,0)="            DESCRIPTION:    This points to the 3.9 (Message) file entry of"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,381,0)="                            the message being transmitted."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,382,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,383,0)="Corrected the spelling of ""included"":"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,384,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,385,0)="4.29991,103   MESSAGES RECEIVED    0;3 NUMBER"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,386,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,387,0)="              INPUT TRANSFORM:K:+X'=X!(X>9999999)!(X<0)!(X?.E1"".""1N.N) X"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,388,0)="              HELP-PROMPT:  Type a Number between 0 and 9999999, 0 Decimal"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,389,0)="                            Digits"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,390,0)="              DESCRIPTION:  Messages received are included here even if"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,391,0)="                            they are received only to be forwarded on to"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,392,0)="                            another site.  Also included are responses and"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,393,0)="                            messages received previously (as long as they"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,394,0)="                            have additional recipients)."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,395,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,396,0)="Increased the length of the following fields:"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,397,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,398,0)="4.29991,104   CHARACTERS SENT      0;4 NUMBER"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,399,0)="              INPUT TRANSFORM:K:+X'=X!(X>999999999)!(X<0)!(X?.E1"".""1.N) X"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,400,0)="              HELP-PROMPT:  Type a number between 0 and 999999999, 0"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,401,0)="                            Decimal Digits"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,402,0)="              DESCRIPTION:  This is a count of the number of characters in"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,403,0)="                            the messages sent."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,404,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,405,0)="4.29991,105   CHARACTERS RECEIVED  0;5 NUMBER"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,406,0)="              INPUT TRANSFORM:K:+X'=X!(X>999999999)!(X<0)!(X?.E1"".""1.N) X"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,407,0)="              HELP-PROMPT:  Type a number between 0 and 999999999, 0"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,408,0)="                            Decimal Digits"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,409,0)="              DESCRIPTION:  This is a count of the number of characters in"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,410,0)="                            the messages received."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,411,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,412,0)="4.29991,106   LINES SENT           0;6 NUMBER"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,413,0)="              INPUT TRANSFORM:K:+X'=X!(X>99999999)!(X<0)!(X?.E1"".""1.N) X"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,414,0)="              HELP-PROMPT:  Type a number between 0 and 99999999, 0 Decimal"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,415,0)="                            Digits"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,416,0)="              DESCRIPTION:  This is a count of the number of lines in the"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,417,0)="                            message sent."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,418,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,419,0)="4.29991,107   LINES RECEIVED       0;7 NUMBER"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,420,0)="              INPUT TRANSFORM:K:+X'=X!(X>99999999)!(X<0)!(X?.E1"".""1.N) X"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,421,0)="              HELP-PROMPT:  Type a number between 0 and 99999999, 0 Decimal"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,422,0)="                            Digits"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,423,0)="              DESCRIPTION:  This is a count of the number of lines in the"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,424,0)="                            messages received."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,425,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,426,0)="f. Add fields to MAILMAN SITE PARAMETERS file 4.3"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,427,0)="(See items 1 and 2, above, for more info.)"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,428,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,429,0)="STANDARD DATA DICTIONARY #4.3 -- MAILMAN SITE PARAMETERS FILE"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,430,0)="STORED IN ^XMB(1,"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,431,0)="DATA        NAME            GLOBAL        DATA"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,432,0)="ELEMENT     TITLE           LOCATION      TYPE"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,433,0)="---------------------------------------------------------------------------"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,434,0)="4.3,8.22    DNS AWARE       NETWORK;2 SET"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,435,0)="                            '0' FOR NO;"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,436,0)="                            '1' FOR YES;"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,437,0)="            HELP-PROMPT:    Should MailMan use DNS to look up IP addresses?"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,438,0)="            DESCRIPTION:    In order for MailMan to be DNS aware, the site"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,439,0)="                            must have installed the requisite Kernel"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,440,0)="                            patches for DNS."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,441,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,442,0)="                            If you answer 'no', MailMan will use the IP"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,443,0)="                            addresses in the domain scripts."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,444,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,445,0)="                            If you answer 'yes', MailMan will use the IP"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,446,0)="                            addresses in the domain scripts, but if they"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,447,0)="                            fail, or don't exist, MailMan will use DNS to"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,448,0)="                            ascertain other IP addresses to try.  MailMan"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,449,0)="                            will replace failed script IP address with the"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,450,0)="                            successful DNS IP address."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,451,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,452,0)="4.3,8.23  TCP/IP COMMUNICATIONS PROTOCOL NETWORK;3"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,453,0)="                            POINTER TO COMMUNICATIONS PROTOCOL FILE (#3.4)"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,454,0)="                            (Required)"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,455,0)="          HELP-PROMPT:      Which protocol shall be used for TCP/IP?"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,456,0)="          DESCRIPTION:      For TCP/IP connections, the scripts (the TEXT"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,457,0)="                            field, 2, in the TRANSMISSION SCRIPT multiple,"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,458,0)="                            4, of the DOMAIN file, 4.2) are ignored."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,459,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,460,0)="                            We identify the TCP/IP transmission scripts in"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,461,0)="                            file 4.2 by the TYPE field, 1.2, within the"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,462,0)="                            TRANSMISSION SCRIPT multiple.  Those whose TYPE"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,463,0)="                            is 'SMTP', 'TCPCHAN', or null are considered"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,464,0)="                            TCP/IP transmission scripts."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,465,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,466,0)="                            We can ignore the scripts, because they are"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,467,0)="                            standard."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,468,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,469,0)="                            Here's an example of one for FORUM:"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,470,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,471,0)="                            O H=DOMAIN.EXT,P=TCP/IP-MAILMAN"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,472,0)="                            C TCPCHAN-SOCKET25-VXD"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,473,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,474,0)="                            In this script, the TCP/IP-MAILMAN refers to"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,475,0)="                            the communications protocol to use.  This field"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,476,0)="                            should point to the communications protocol in"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,477,0)="                            file 3.4 that should be used for TCP/IP"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,478,0)="                            connections."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,479,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,480,0)="4.3,8.24  TCP/IP TRANSMISSION SCRIPT NETWORK;4"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,481,0)="                            POINTER TO TRANSMISSION SCRIPT FILE (#4.6)"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,482,0)="                            (Required)"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,483,0)="          HELP-PROMPT:      Which script shall be used for TCP/IP?"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,484,0)="          DESCRIPTION:      For TCP/IP connections, the scripts (the TEXT"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,485,0)="                            field, 2, in the TRANSMISSION SCRIPT multiple,"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,486,0)="                            4, of the DOMAIN file, 4.2) are ignored."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,487,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,488,0)="                            We identify the TCP/IP transmission scripts in"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,489,0)="                            file 4.2 by the TYPE field, 1.2, within the"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,490,0)="                            TRANSMISSION SCRIPT multiple.  Those whose TYPE"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,491,0)="                            is 'SMTP', 'TCPCHAN', or null are considered"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,492,0)="                            TCP/IP transmission scripts."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,493,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,494,0)="                            We can ignore the scripts, because they are"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,495,0)="                            standard."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,496,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,497,0)="                            Here's an example of one for FORUM:"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,498,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,499,0)="                            O H=DOMAIN.EXT,P=TCP/IP-MAILMAN"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,500,0)="                            C TCPCHAN-SOCKET25-VXD"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,501,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,502,0)="                            In this script, the TCPCHAN-SOCKET25-VXD refers"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,503,0)="                            to the transmission script to use.  This field"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,504,0)="                            should point to the transmission script in file"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,505,0)="                            4.6 that should be used for TCP/IP connections."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,506,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,507,0)="NOTE: This release should be installed after hours, when user activity is at"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,508,0)="a minimum.  Your site should be patched through MailMan patch XM*7.1*198."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,509,0)="It is also expected that Kernel 8.0 and FileMan 22.0 are installed and are"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,510,0)="reasonably up-to-date (through patches XU*8.0*216 and DI*22.0*68,"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,511,0)="respectively).  It is up to you to check this.  KIDS will not."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,512,0)="Installation will take approximately 5 minutes."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,513,0)="============================================================================ "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,514,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,515,0)="ROUTINES:"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,516,0)="The second line of the routines now look like:"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,517,0)="       ;;8.0;MailMan;;Jun 28, 2002"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,518,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,519,0)="Routine list and checksums can be found in routines XMNTEG and XMNTEG0."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,520,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,521,0)="==========================================================================="
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,522,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,523,0)="INSTALLATION:"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,524,0)="NOTES:"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,525,0)="a. This release should be installed after hours, when user activity is at a"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,526,0)="minimum.  Your site should be patched through MailMan patch XM*7.1*198.  It"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,527,0)="is also expected that Kernel 8.0 and FileMan 22.0 are installed and are"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,528,0)="reasonably up-to-date (through patches XU*8.0*216 and DI*22.0*68,"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,529,0)="respectively).  It is up to you to check this.  KIDS will not."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,530,0)="b. Installation will take approximately 5 minutes."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,531,0)="c. DSM sites running SlotMaster may see routine mapping problems with ZSLOT"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,532,0)="users, even after disabling mapping.  Therefore, such sites should consider"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,533,0)="the following steps:"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,534,0)="- Shutdown ZSLOT on all nodes."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,535,0)="- Disable all mapped XM routines."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,536,0)="- Install this release."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,537,0)="- Enter manual edits."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,538,0)="- Rebuild mapped routine sets."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,539,0)="- Shutdown/restart all DSM configurations."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,540,0)="An alternative to the above steps is to disable all mapped XM routines about"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,541,0)="a week before you load this release.  That way you wouldn't need to shutdown"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,542,0)="ZSLOT or shutdown/restart DSM configurations."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,543,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,544,0)="1.  Users may NOT be on the system during installation of this release."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,545,0)="2.  DSM Sites: disable mapping for all MailMan routines."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,546,0)="3.  Retrieve the MailMan 8.0 KIDS build (XM8.KID) via FTP from one of the"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,547,0)="    Anonymous Directories by following the instructions in the MailMan 8.0"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,548,0)="    Release Message."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,549,0)="4.  On the KIDS:Installation menu, use the following option to load the"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,550,0)="    build into a Transport Global on your system:"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,551,0)="       Load a Distribution"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,552,0)=" Enter a Host File: E:\INETPUB\FTPROOT\XM8.KID or USR$:[USER.NAME]XM8.KID"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,553,0)="                    ==========================    ======================="
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,554,0)=" (How you answer the above prompt depends on the system you're on and where"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,555,0)=" you placed the build you retrieved in the previous step.  The answers"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,556,0)=" shown are examples only.)"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,557,0)="5.  Users should not be on the system."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,558,0)="    Place TaskMan in a WAIT state."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,559,0)="    You may also wish to shut down HL7 filers/LLP's and other background"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,560,0)="    tasks which may attempt to create new messages."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,561,0)="6.  On the Manage MailMan:Local Delivery Management menu, use the following"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,562,0)="    option to stop the background filer:"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,563,0)="       STOP background filer"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,564,0)="Are you sure you want Background Filers to stop delivering mail? No// YES"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,565,0)="                                                                      ==="
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,566,0)="<< Background Filers will stop soon. >>"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,567,0)="7.  On the KIDS:Installation menu, use the following options to install the"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,568,0)="    Transport Global:"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,569,0)="       Verify Checksums in Transport Global"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,570,0)="       Print Transport Global"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,571,0)="       Compare Transport Global to Current System"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,572,0)="       Backup a Transport Global"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,573,0)="       Install Package(s)"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,574,0)=" Select INSTALL NAME:   MAILMAN 8.0   Loaded from Distribution  <date/time>"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,575,0)="                        ==========="
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,576,0)="Install Questions for MAILMAN 8.0"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,577,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,578,0)="Incoming Files:"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,579,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,580,0)="   3.4       COMMUNICATIONS PROTOCOL"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,581,0)="Note:  You already have the 'COMMUNICATIONS PROTOCOL' File."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,582,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,583,0)="   3.6       BULLETIN"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,584,0)="Note:  You already have the 'BULLETIN' File."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,585,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,586,0)="   3.7       MAILBOX"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,587,0)="Note:  You already have the 'MAILBOX' File."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,588,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,589,0)="   3.73      MESSAGES TO BE NEW AT A LATER DATE"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,590,0)="Note:  You already have the 'MESSAGES TO BE NEW AT A LATER DATE' File."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,591,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,592,0)="   3.8       MAIL GROUP"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,593,0)="Note:  You already have the 'MAIL GROUP' File."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,594,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,595,0)="   3.816     DISTRIBUTION LIST"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,596,0)="Note:  You already have the 'DISTRIBUTION LIST' File."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,597,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,598,0)="   3.9       MESSAGE"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,599,0)="Note:  You already have the 'MESSAGE' File."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,600,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,601,0)="   4.2       DOMAIN"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,602,0)="Note:  You already have the 'DOMAIN' File."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,603,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,604,0)="   4.281     INTER-UCI TRANSFER"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,605,0)="Note:  You already have the 'INTER-UCI TRANSFER' File."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,606,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,607,0)="   4.2996    INTERNET SUFFIX"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,608,0)="Note:  You already have the 'INTERNET SUFFIX' File."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,609,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,610,0)="   4.2997    REMOTE USER DIRECTORY"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,611,0)="Note:  You already have the 'REMOTE USER DIRECTORY' File."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,612,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,613,0)="   4.2998    MESSAGE DELIVERY STATS"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,614,0)="Note:  You already have the 'MESSAGE DELIVERY STATS' File."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,615,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,616,0)="   4.2999    MESSAGE STATISTICS"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,617,0)="Note:  You already have the 'MESSAGE STATISTICS' File."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,618,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,619,0)="   4.3       MAILMAN SITE PARAMETERS"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,620,0)="Note:  You already have the 'MAILMAN SITE PARAMETERS' File."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,621,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,622,0)="   4.4       MAILMAN TIME ZONE"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,623,0)="Note:  You already have the 'MAILMAN TIME ZONE' File."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,624,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,625,0)="   4.501     NETWORK SENDERS REJECTED"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,626,0)="Note:  You already have the 'NETWORK SENDERS REJECTED' File."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,627,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,628,0)="   4.6       TRANSMISSION SCRIPT"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,629,0)="Note:  You already have the 'TRANSMISSION SCRIPT' File."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,630,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,631,0)="Incoming Mail Groups:"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,632,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,633,0)="Enter the Coordinator for Mail Group 'XM SUPER SEARCH': <Your site's ISO>"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,634,0)="                                                        ================="
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,635,0)="Want KIDS to Rebuild Menu Trees Upon Completion of Install? YES// YES"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,636,0)="                                                                  ==="
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,637,0)="Want KIDS to INHIBIT LOGONs during the install? YES// YES"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,638,0)="                                                      ==="
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,639,0)="Want to DISABLE Scheduled Options, Menu Options, and Protocols? YES// NO"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,640,0)="                                                                      =="
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,641,0)=" Enter the Device you want to print the Install messages."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,642,0)=" You can queue the install by enter a 'Q' at the device prompt."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,643,0)=" Enter a '^' to abort the install."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,644,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,645,0)=" DEVICE: HOME// <Don't queue this>"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,646,0)="                ------------------"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,647,0)="8. Remove TaskMan from WAIT State.  If you shut down HL7 filers/LLPs or"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,648,0)="   any other background tasks, you may restart them."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,649,0)="9. Start the background filer."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,650,0)="    On the Manage MailMan:Local Delivery Management menu, use the following"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,651,0)="    option to start the background filer:"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,652,0)="       START background filer"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,653,0)="   << Background Filers will start soon. >>"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,654,0)="10. Ensure that the new fields in file 4.3, MAILMAN SITE PARAMETERS, are"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,655,0)="correctly entered:"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,656,0)="     8.22  DNS AWARE                        Yes or No?"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,657,0)="     8.23  TCP/IP COMMUNICATIONS PROTOCOL   Points to file 3.4"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,658,0)="     8.24  TCP/IP TRANSMISSION SCRIPT       Points to file 4.6"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,659,0)="11. DSM Sites: If your site maps routines, then rebuild your map set."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,660,0)="    Many routines have become obsolete and should be removed from the map"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,661,0)="    set.  Here is a new recommended list of routines to map, should your"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,662,0)="    site choose to map routines:"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,663,0)="    Minimum                           Additional"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,664,0)="    -------                           ----------"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,665,0)="    XM                                XMC1A,XMC1B"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,666,0)="    XMC1                              XMK*"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,667,0)="    XMJ*                              XML"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,668,0)="    XMR                               XMR1,XMR2,XMR3*"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,669,0)="    XMS                               XMS1,XMS2,XMS3"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,670,0)="                                      XMT*"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,671,0)="                                      XMVVITAE"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,672,0)="                                      XMXADDR*"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,673,0)="                                      XMXSEC*"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,674,0)="                                      XMXUTIL*"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,1,675,0)="==========================================================================="
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",0)="^9.4901^43^43"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,0)="4 SEQ #1^3020906^1"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,0)="^^57^57^3020906"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,1,0)="Patch XM*8.0*4"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,2,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,3,0)="NOIS: ISD-0802-72865"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,4,0)="Test Site:  FO-Dallas"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,5,0)="Two function entry points in a Poller routine do not have the required open"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,6,0)="and close parentheses.  This patch fixes that."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,7,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,8,0)="NOTE: This patch may be installed at any time. It has no patch prerequisites,"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,9,0)="except, of course, that MailMan 8.0 be installed."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,10,0)="============================================================================ "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,11,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,12,0)="ROUTINES:"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,13,0)="The second line of the routine now looks like:"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,14,0)="       ;;8.0;MailMan;**[patch list]**;Jun 28, 2002"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,15,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,16,0)="              Before          After"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,17,0)="Name          Checksum        Checksum        Patch List"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,18,0)="------------------------------------------------------------------"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,19,0)="XMCP           2311480         2312858        4"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,20,0)="* Checksums produced by CHECK^XTSUMBLD"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,21,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,22,0)="This patch introduces no new routines."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,23,0)="==========================================================================="
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,24,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,25,0)="INSTALLATION:"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,26,0)="NOTE: This patch may be installed at any time. It has no patch prerequisites,"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,27,0)="except, of course, that MailMan 8.0 be installed."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,28,0)="1.  Users may be on the system during installation of this patch."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,29,0)="2.  DSM Sites: If any of these routines is mapped, disable mapping for the "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,30,0)="    affected routine(s).  "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,31,0)="3.  On the PackMan menu, use the 'INSTALL/CHECK MESSAGE' option.  This loads "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,32,0)="    the patch into a Transport Global on your system.  "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,33,0)="4.  Users may be on the system.  You do not need to stop TaskMan"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,34,0)="    or the background filer."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,35,0)="5.  On the KIDS:Installation menu, use the following options to install the"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,36,0)="    Transport Global:"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,37,0)="       Verify Checksums in Transport Global"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,38,0)="       Print Transport Global"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,39,0)="       Compare Transport Global to Current System"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,40,0)="       Backup a Transport Global"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,41,0)="       Install Package(s)"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,42,0)=" Select INSTALL NAME:    XM*8.0*4     Loaded from Distribution  <date/time>"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,43,0)="                         ========"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,44,0)=" Install Questions for XM*8.0*4"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,45,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,46,0)=" Want KIDS to INHIBIT LOGONs during the install? YES// NO"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,47,0)="                                                       =="
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,48,0)=" Want to DISABLE Scheduled Options, Menu Options, and Protocols? YES// NO"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,49,0)="                                                                       =="
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,50,0)=" Enter the Device you want to print the Install messages."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,51,0)=" You can queue the install by enter a 'Q' at the device prompt."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,52,0)=" Enter a '^' to abort the install."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,53,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,54,0)=" DEVICE: HOME// <You may queue it if you wish>"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,55,0)="                ------------------------------"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,56,0)="6.  DSM Sites: After patch has installed, rebuild your map set, if necessary."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",1,1,57,0)="==========================================================================="
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,0)="3 SEQ #2^3020927^1"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,0)="^^134^134^3020927"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,1,0)="      * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * *"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,2,0)="                             ATTENTION!"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,3,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,4,0)="      This patch contains a new field, which you should populate."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,5,0)="      * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * *"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,6,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,7,0)="Patch XM*8.0*3"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,8,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,9,0)="1.  NOIS: LAS-0802-62501"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,10,0)="Test Site:  Las Vegas, NV"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,11,0)="The communications testing routine, XMCDNT, distributed with MailMan 8.0"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,12,0)="was an old version which does not work.  This patch provides a version"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,13,0)="that works."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,14,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,15,0)="2.  This patch also adds a new field to the MAILMAN SITE PARAMETERS (#4.3)"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,16,0)="file.  You should fill it in with the DEVICE you normally use for your"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,17,0)="TCP/IP scripts in DOMAIN (#4.2) file.  With the addition of this new field,"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,18,0)="it is now possible to distribute new domains automatically, rather than"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,19,0)="having you enter them manually.  Here's the new field:"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,20,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,21,0)="STANDARD DATA DICTIONARY #4.3 -- MAILMAN SITE PARAMETERS FILE"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,22,0)="STORED IN ^XMB(1,"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,23,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,24,0)="DATA       NAME                  GLOBAL        DATA"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,25,0)="ELEMENT    TITLE                 LOCATION      TYPE"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,26,0)="----------------------------------------------------------------------------"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,27,0)="4.3,8.25   TCP/IP DEVICE          NETWORK;5 FREE TEXT"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,28,0)="           INPUT TRANSFORM:  K:$L(X)>30!($L(X)<1) X I $D(X) S %ZIS=""NQRS"",IO"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,29,0)="                             P=X D ^%ZIS K:POP X S:$D(X) X=ION W:$D(X) "" Sto"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,30,0)="                             red internally as "",X D ^%ZISC S IOP=""HOME"" D ^"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,31,0)="                             %ZIS K IOP,%ZIS"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,32,0)="           HELP-PROMPT:      Which device shall be used for TCP/IP?"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,33,0)="           DESCRIPTION:      For TCP/IP connections, the physical"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,34,0)="                             link/device to be used is usually standard -"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,35,0)="                             some sort of NULL device.  This field is a"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,36,0)="                             free-text pointer to that device in the DEVICE"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,37,0)="                             (#3.5) file."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,38,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,39,0)="                             The device pointed to by this field will be"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,40,0)="                             used for a TCP/IP connection if, in the DOMAIN"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,41,0)="                             (#4.2) file, the device field is null in both"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,42,0)="                             of the following fields:"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,43,0)="                              - PHYSICAL LINK / DEVICE (#1.3) field of the"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,44,0)="                             TRANSMISSION SCRIPT (#4) multiple"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,45,0)="                              - PHYSICAL LINK DEVICE (#17) field"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,46,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,47,0)="                             For more information, see the PHYSICAL LINK"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,48,0)="                             DEVICE (#17) field, in the DOMAIN (#4.2) file."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,49,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,50,0)="           NOTES:            XXXX--CAN'T BE ALTERED EXCEPT BY PROGRAMMER"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,51,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,52,0)="3.  Finally, this patch corrects the descriptions in two fields:"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,53,0)="- MAIL GROUP (#3.8) file, MEMBER GROUPS (#11) field - invalid information"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,54,0)="deleted"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,55,0)="- DOMAIN (#4.2) file, PHYSICAL LINK DEVICE (#17) field - information about"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,56,0)="default values corrected"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,57,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,58,0)="NOTE: This patch should be installed during off hours, when user activity"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,59,0)="is at a minimum.  It has no patch prerequisites, except, of course, that"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,60,0)="MailMan 8.0 be installed.  Installation will take less than 1 minute."
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,61,0)="============================================================================ "
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,62,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,63,0)="ROUTINES:"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,64,0)="The second line of the routine now looks like:"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,65,0)="       ;;8.0;MailMan;**[patch list]**;Jun 28, 2002"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,66,0)=""
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,67,0)="              Before          After"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,68,0)="Name          Checksum        Checksum        Patch List"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,69,0)="------------------------------------------------------------------"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,70,0)="XMCDNT          401792          659873        3"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,71,0)="XMKPR1         5051210         5167213        3"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,72,0)="* Checksums produced by CHECK^XTSUMBLD"
^DIC(9.4,4,22,2,"PAH",2,1,73,0)=""
