^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,92,0)="Installation Instructions: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,93,0)="=========================="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,94,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,95,0)="    1.  Two of the routines in this patch, DIC and DIOU, are usually"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,96,0)="mapped on systems that allow mapping, AXP sites will need to disable"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,97,0)="mapping for these routines."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,98,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,99,0)="    2.  Use the 'INSTALL/CHECK MESSAGE' option on the PackMan menu. This"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,100,0)="option will load the KIDS package onto your system.  The environment check"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,101,0)="routine DIENV will run automatically."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,102,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,103,0)="    DIENV is sent in order to prevent the inadvertent queueing of the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,104,0)="installation of this patch."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,105,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,106,0)="    3.  The patch has now been loaded into a transport global on your"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,107,0)="system. You now need to use KIDS to install the transport global."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,108,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,109,0)="    4.  On the KIDS menu, under the 'Installation' menu, use the following"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,110,0)="options: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,111,0)="           Print Transport Global "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,112,0)="           Compare Transport Global to Current System "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,113,0)="           Verify Checksums in Transport Global "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,114,0)="           Backup a Transport Global "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,115,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,116,0)="    5.  Inhibit users from login into the system.  Some of the routines"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,117,0)="being patched are used all over FileMan.  Having users on the system"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,118,0)="during installation could result in incomplete records or CLOBBER errors."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,119,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,120,0)="    6.  AXP Sites - Place Taskman in a WAIT State.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,121,0)="        MSM Sites - STOP Taskman.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,122,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,123,0)="    7.  On the KIDS menu, under the 'Installation' menu, use the following"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,124,0)="option: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,125,0)="           Select Installation Option: Install Package(s)"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,126,0)="           Select INSTALL NAME: DI*21.0*8 "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,127,0)="                                =========="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,128,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,129,0)="           Answer 'NO' to 'Want to DISABLE Scheduled Options, Menu"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,130,0)="Options, and Protocols?"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,131,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,132,0)="    8.  MSM Sites - Answer YES to the question 'Want to MOVE routines to"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,133,0)="other CPUs?'."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,134,0)="     Then enter the names of your other Compute and Print server(s)."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,135,0)="     NOTE: You will see the following message:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,136,0)="       TASKMAN is not running. You must run the routine XPDCPU in the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,137,0)="production UCI for each of the VOLUME SETS you have listed once the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,138,0)="installation starts!!"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,139,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,140,0)="    9.  MSM Sites - After the installation begins, you will be prompted to"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,141,0)="enter a DEVICE.  After the device has been entered, KIDS will pause.  At"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,142,0)="this time, you need to go to each of the CPUs that were entered in step 8,"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,143,0)="and start a job that will allow them to receive the patched routines.  On"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,144,0)="each one, you do the following:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,145,0)="    a) >D ^XUP    This will set up the programming environment."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,146,0)="        ======"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,147,0)="    b) >D ^XPDCPU  This starts the job that receives the patched routines."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,148,0)="        ========="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,149,0)="    c) Select INSTALL NAME: DI*21.0*8 "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,150,0)="                            ========="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,151,0)="The installation will then continue.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,152,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,153,0)="    10.  AXP Sites - After the patch has been installed, rebuild your map"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,154,0)="set. "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,155,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,156,0)="    11.  AXP Sites - Remove Taskman from WAIT State.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,157,0)="         MSM Sites - Restart Taskman.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,158,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",4,1,159,0)="    12.  Enable user's login.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,0)="31^2961015^1"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,0)="^^102^102^2961015"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,1,0)="This patch fixes the following problem:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,2,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,3,0)="1. PCE sites have reported sluggish performance. Skip Ormsby at "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,4,0)="Clarksburg has tracked this to the activities of the FileMan Lister when "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,5,0)="it is used on the ICD9 file. The ICD9 file lacks a B index, and the "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,6,0)="Lister recreates and later deletes a temporary B index before each call "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,7,0)="for that file. The Lister should only do that when asked to use the "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,8,0)="missing B index, but always does due to a bug introduced by DI*21*8. "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,9,0)="After installing this patch, PCE's performance should noticeably improve."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,10,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,11,0)="Routine Summary:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,12,0)="================"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,13,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,14,0)="The following routine is included in this patch. The second line of"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,15,0)="this routines now looks like:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,16,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,17,0)="  <tab>;;21.0;VA FileMan;**[patch list]**;Dec 28, 1994"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,18,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,19,0)="CHECK^XTSUMBLD results:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,20,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,21,0)="  Routine Name   Before Patch    After Patch     Patch List"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,22,0)="  ============   ============    ===========     =========="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,23,0)="  DICU1          5260389         5299469         17,8,31"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,24,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,25,0)="Installation Instructions:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,26,0)="=========================="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,27,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,28,0)="    1.  AXP Sites - Although routine mapping is NOT recommended for this"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,29,0)="routine, your mapped set may include routines not usually recommended for"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,30,0)="mapping. If this routine is in your local map set, disable mapping for it"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,31,0)="before proceeding with the installation, and rebuild your map set"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,32,0)="afterward before reactivating TaskMan."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,33,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,34,0)="    2.  Use the 'INSTALL/CHECK MESSAGE' option on the PackMan menu. This"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,35,0)="option will load the KIDS package onto your system.  The environment"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,36,0)="check routine, DIENV, will run automatically."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,37,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,38,0)="    DIENV is sent to prevent the inadvertent queuing of the installation"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,39,0)="of this patch."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,40,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,41,0)="    3.  The patch has now been loaded into a transport global on your"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,42,0)="system. You now need to use KIDS to install the transport global."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,43,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,44,0)="    4.  On the KIDS menu, under the 'Installation' menu, use the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,45,0)="following options:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,46,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,47,0)="           Print Transport Global"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,48,0)="           Compare Transport Global to Current System"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,49,0)="           Verify Checksums in Transport Global"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,50,0)="           Backup a Transport Global"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,51,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,52,0)="    5.  Inhibit users from login into the system.  The consequence of"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,53,0)="having users on the system while installing this patch is that users"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,54,0)="looking up records will get a 'source routine edited' error.  Although"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,55,0)="this routine is not heavily used in DHCP applications yet, it is"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,56,0)="used increasingly in new application such as PCE."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,57,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,58,0)="    6.  AXP Sites - Place Taskman in a WAIT State."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,59,0)="        MSM Sites - STOP Taskman."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,60,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,61,0)="    7.  On the KIDS menu, under the 'Installation' menu, use the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,62,0)="following option:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,63,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,64,0)="              Select Installation Option: Install Package(s)"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,65,0)="              Select INSTALL NAME: DI*21.0*31"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,66,0)="                                   =========="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,67,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,68,0)="              Answer 'NO' to 'Want to DISABLE Scheduled Options, Menu"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,69,0)="Options, and Protocols?"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,70,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,71,0)="    8.  MSM Sites - Answer YES to the question 'Want to MOVE routines to"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,72,0)="other CPUs?'."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,73,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,74,0)="     Then enter the names of your other Compute and Print server(s)."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,75,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,76,0)="     NOTE: You will then see the following message:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,77,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,78,0)="     TASKMAN is not running. You must run the routine XPDCPU in the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,79,0)="production UCI for each of the VOLUME SETS you have listed once the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,80,0)="installation starts!!"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,81,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,82,0)="    9.  MSM Sites - After the installation begins, you will be prompted"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,83,0)="to enter a DEVICE.  After the device has been entered, KIDS will pause."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,84,0)="At this time, you need to go to each of the CPUs that were entered in"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,85,0)="step 8, and start a job that will allow them to receive the patched"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,86,0)="routines.  On each one, you do the following:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,87,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,88,0)="    a) >D ^XUP    This will set up the programming environment."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,89,0)="        ======"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,90,0)="    b) >D ^XPDCPU This starts the job that receives the patched routines."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,91,0)="        ========="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,92,0)="    c) Select INSTALL NAME: DI*21.0*31"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,93,0)="                            =========="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,94,0)="The installation will then continue."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,95,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,96,0)="    10.  AXP Sites - After the patch has been installed and IF you"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,97,0)="disabled your map set (step 1 above), rebuild your map set."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,98,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,99,0)="    11.  AXP Sites - Remove Taskman from WAIT State."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,100,0)="         MSM Sites - Restart Taskman."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,101,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",5,1,102,0)="    12.  Enable users' login."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,0)="18^2961030^1"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,0)="^^105^105^2961030^"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,1,0)="This patch fixes 2 problems:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,2,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,3,0)="1. This patch fixes a very specific problem with converting time at "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,4,0)="midnight.  The related NOIS is BTM-1095-13315.  (DIDTC)"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,5,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,6,0)="2. Before this patch, %DT was accepting input that consisted of 8 or more"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,7,0)="numerics as long as the first 7 digits formed a valid date in FM internal"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,8,0)="format and %DT contained a ""T"".  For example, %DT interpreted 604011422 as"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,9,0)="Jan 14, 2304.  This patch makes %DT reject such input.  The related NOIS's"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,10,0)="are KNX-0396-41289 and WCO-0195-50090.  (DIDT)"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,11,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,12,0)="Routine Summary:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,13,0)="================"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,14,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,15,0)="The following routines are included in this patch.  The second line of"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,16,0)="these routines now look like:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,17,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,18,0)="  <tab>;;21.0;VA FileMan;**[patch list]**;Dec 28, 1994 "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,19,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,20,0)="CHECK^XTSUMBLD results: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,21,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,22,0)="  Routine Name   Before Patch    After Patch     Patch List "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,23,0)="  ============   ============    ===========     =========="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,24,0)="  DIDT           6670456         6692350         18"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,25,0)="  DIDTC          7237147         7334026         18"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,26,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,27,0)="Installation Instructions: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,28,0)="=========================="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,29,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,30,0)="    1.  The routines %DT and %DTC are usually mapped on systems that allow"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,31,0)="mapping.  AXP sites will need to disable mapping for these routines."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,32,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,33,0)="    2.  Use the 'INSTALL/CHECK MESSAGE' option on the PackMan menu. This "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,34,0)="option will load the KIDS package onto your system.  The environment check"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,35,0)="routine DIENV will run automatically."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,36,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,37,0)="    DIENV is sent in order to prevent the inadvertent queueing of the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,38,0)="installation of this patch."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,39,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,40,0)="    3.  The patch has now been loaded into a transport global on your"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,41,0)="system. You now need to use KIDS to install the transport global."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,42,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,43,0)="    4.  On the KIDS menu, under the 'Installation' menu, use the following"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,44,0)="options: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,45,0)="           Print Transport Global "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,46,0)="           Compare Transport Global to Current System "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,47,0)="           Verify Checksums in Transport Global "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,48,0)="           Backup a Transport Global "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,49,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,50,0)="    5.  Inhibit users' login into the system.  If users are on the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,51,0)="system, you run the risk of getting incorrect 'date with time'"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,52,0)="values and/or CLOBBER errors. Due to the high usage of these routines in"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,53,0)="all of DHCP code, it is highly recommended that users get off the system"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,54,0)="when installing this patch."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,55,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,56,0)="    6.  AXP Sites - Place Taskman in a WAIT State.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,57,0)="        MSM Sites - STOP Taskman.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,58,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,59,0)="    7.  On the KIDS menu, under the 'Installation' menu, use the following"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,60,0)="option: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,61,0)="           Select Installation Option: Install Package(s)"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,62,0)="           Select INSTALL NAME: DI*21.0*18"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,63,0)="                                =========="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,64,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,65,0)="           Answer 'NO' to 'Want to DISABLE Scheduled Options, Menu"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,66,0)="Options, and Protocols?"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,67,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,68,0)="    8.  MSM Sites - Answer YES to the question 'Want to MOVE routines to"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,69,0)="other CPUs?'."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,70,0)="     Then enter the names of your other Compute and Print server(s).  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,71,0)="     NOTE: You will then see the following message: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,72,0)="       TASKMAN is not running. You must run the routine XPDCPU in the "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,73,0)="production UCI for each of the VOLUME SETS you have listed once the "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,74,0)="installation starts!! "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,75,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,76,0)="    9.  MSM Sites - After the installation begins, you will be prompted to"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,77,0)="enter a DEVICE.  After the device has been entered, KIDS will pause.  At"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,78,0)="this time, you need to go to each of the CPUs that were entered in step 8,"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,79,0)="and start a job that will allow them to receive the patched routines.  On"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,80,0)="each one, you do the following:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,81,0)="    a) >D ^XUP    This will set up the programming environment.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,82,0)="       ======"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,83,0)="    b) >D ^XPDCPU  This starts the job that receives the patched routines."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,84,0)="        ========="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,85,0)="    c) Select INSTALL NAME: DI*21.0*18"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,86,0)="                            =========="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,87,0)="The installation will then continue.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,88,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,89,0)="    10.  After installing this patch, you need to move these routines"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,90,0)="from your production account to the manager account:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,91,0)="       DIDT, DIDTC"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,92,0)="       and in the manager account rename"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,93,0)="       DIDT to %DT"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,94,0)="       DIDTC to %DTC"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,95,0)="      "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,96,0)="       Example: ZL DIDT ZS %DT"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,97,0)="                ZL DIDTC ZS %DTC"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,98,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,99,0)="    11.  AXP Sites - After the patch has been installed, rebuild your map"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,100,0)="set.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,101,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,102,0)="    12.  AXP Sites - Remove Taskman from WAIT State.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,103,0)="         MSM Sites - Restart Taskman."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,104,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",6,1,105,0)="    13.  Enable users' login.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,0)="33^2961224^1"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,0)="^^103^103^2961224"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,1,0)="This patch fixes a few bugs related to word-processing fields:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,2,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,3,0)="1.  This patch fixes problems with the 'Transfer Text' Edit option."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,4,0)="(DIWE3,DIWE5, NOIS BRX-0495-10374))"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,5,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,6,0)="2.  This patch fixes the header that gets printed when using the Print"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,7,0)="option from the Line Editor.  (DIWE4)"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,8,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,9,0)="3.  This patch prevents the printing of duplicate last lines when the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,10,0)="NOWRAP function is used.  (DIWP, TUA-1094-30461)"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,11,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,12,0)="4.  This patch prevents an infinite loop from happening when DIWL=0 and a"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,13,0)="call to ^DIWW is made.  (DIWP, NOIS BRX-0995-11453)"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,14,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,15,0)="5.  This patch allows the 'Transfer Text' Edit option from an EN^DIWE"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,16,0)="call provided that the text being edited is a FM word-processing field."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,17,0)="(DIWE)"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,18,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,19,0)=" Routine Summary: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,20,0)=" ================"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,21,0)="  The following routines are included in this patch.  The second line of"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,22,0)="these routines now look like:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,23,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,24,0)="  <tab>;;21.0;VA FileMan;**[patch list]**;Dec 28, 1994 "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,25,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,26,0)="  CHECK^XTSUMBLD results: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,27,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,28,0)="  Routine Name   Before Patch    After Patch     Patch List "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,29,0)="  ============   ============    ===========     =========="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,30,0)="  DIWE           6067379         5827467         33"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,31,0)="  DIWE3          8633894         12713274        33"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,32,0)="  DIWE4          9748975         9685833         33"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,33,0)="  DIWE5          8399507         7891987         33"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,34,0)="  DIWP           5103576         5150036         33"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,35,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,36,0)=" Installation Instructions: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,37,0)=" =========================="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,38,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,39,0)="    1.  The routines DIW* are usually mapped on systems that allow"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,40,0)="mapping.  Alpha sites that use routine mapping will need to disable"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,41,0)="mapping for these routines."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,42,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,43,0)="    2.  Use the 'INSTALL/CHECK MESSAGE' option on the PackMan menu. This"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,44,0)="option will load the KIDS package onto your system.  The environment check"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,45,0)="routine DIENV will run automatically."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,46,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,47,0)="    The Environment Check routine, DIENV, is sent in order to prevent the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,48,0)="inadvertent queueing of the installation of this patch."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,49,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,50,0)="    3.  The patch has now been loaded into a transport global on your"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,51,0)="system. You now need to use KIDS to install the transport global."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,52,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,53,0)="    4.  On the KIDS menu, under the 'Installation' menu, use the following"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,54,0)="options: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,55,0)="           Print Transport Global "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,56,0)="           Compare Transport Global to Current System "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,57,0)="           Verify Checksums in Transport Global "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,58,0)="           Backup a Transport Global "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,59,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,60,0)="    5.  Inhibit users from login into the system.  If users stay on the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,61,0)="system while installing this patch, a hard error may occur while word"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,62,0)="processing fields are printed or edited."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,63,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,64,0)="    6.  AXP Sites - Place Taskman in a WAIT State.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,65,0)="        MSM Sites - STOP Taskman.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,66,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,67,0)="    7.  On the KIDS menu, under the 'Installation' menu, use the following"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,68,0)="option: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,69,0)="           Select Installation Option:  Install Package(s)"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,70,0)="           Select INSTALL NAME: DI*21.0*33"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,71,0)="                                =========="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,72,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,73,0)="           Answer 'NO' to 'Want to DISABLE Scheduled Options, Menu"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,74,0)="Options, and Protocols?"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,75,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,76,0)="    8.  MSM Sites - Answer YES to the question 'Want to MOVE routines to"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,77,0)="other CPUs?'."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,78,0)="    Then enter the names of your other Compute and Print server(s).  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,79,0)="    NOTE: You will then see the following message: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,80,0)="       TASKMAN is not running. You must run the routine XPDCPU in the "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,81,0)="production UCI for each of the VOLUME SETS you have listed once the "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,82,0)="installation starts!! "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,83,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,84,0)="    9.  MSM Sites - After the installation begins, you will be prompted to"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,85,0)="enter a DEVICE.  After the device has been entered, KIDS will pause.  At"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,86,0)="this time, you need to go to each of the CPUs that were entered in step 8,"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,87,0)="and start a job that will allow them to receive the patched routines.  On"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,88,0)="each one, you do the following:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,89,0)="    a) >D ^XUP    This will set up the programming environment.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,90,0)="        ======"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,91,0)="    b) >D ^XPDCPU  This starts the job that receives the patched routines."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,92,0)="        ========="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,93,0)="    c) Select INSTALL NAME: DI*21.0*33"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,94,0)="                            =========="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,95,0)="The installation will then continue.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,96,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,97,0)="    10.  AXP Sites - After the patch has been installed, rebuild your map"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,98,0)="set. "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,99,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,100,0)="    11.  AXP Sites - Remove Taskman from WAIT State.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,101,0)="         MSM Sites - Restart Taskman.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,102,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",7,1,103,0)="    12.  Enable users' login.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,0)="20^2961224^1"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,0)="^^243^243^2961224"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,1,0)="This patch fixes the following problems:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,2,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,3,0)="1.  If a form has no data dictionary fields, but DA happens to be set"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,4,0)="before the ^DDS call, the caller could get the message 'Entry does not"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,5,0)="exist', and the form would fail to run.  (DDS0)"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,6,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,7,0)="2.  If in the Form Editor, you add a data dictionary field to the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,8,0)="form, but at the Field property, you enter a field number that doesn't"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,9,0)="exist, the Form Editor went on to use the ""GL"" nodes in the DD as if they"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,10,0)="were a lookup cross reference, and could pick up the wrong field.  (DDS0)"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,11,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,12,0)="3.  If the last field on a block is uneditable, and you pressed <Up> or"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,13,0)="<PF4> from another block or from the Command Line to this field, the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,14,0)="cursor would appear to be stuck, and would remain in its current location."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,15,0)="(DDS01)"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,16,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,17,0)="4.  Suppose on a form you have a form-only field that is defined as"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,18,0)="'Reachable', which means the user can navigate to the field but can't"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,19,0)="change its value.  If the user tried to change the value, ScreenMan would "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,20,0)="correctly display a message that editing was not allowed.  But instead of "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,21,0)="restoring the original value in the editing window, ScreenMan would"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,22,0)="display the new value entered by the user.  (DDS02)"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,23,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,24,0)="5.  Suppose you have a repeating block that displays pointer-type .01"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,25,0)="fields, and connected to that .01 field you have a pop-up page that"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,26,0)="displays fields in the pointed-to entry.  If one of the .01 field values"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,27,0)="in the repeating block is a dangling pointer, when we pressed return at"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,28,0)="that record, the pop-up page would open up and display the data for the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,29,0)="record we last selected.  This patch prevents the pop-up page from opening"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,30,0)="up when we press return at the dangling pointer.  (DDS1)"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,31,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,32,0)="6.  Before this patch, users were not able to jump to fields with"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,33,0)="executable captions.  This patch allows users to jumps to fields with"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,34,0)="executable captions that are on the current page.  (DDS2, DDSR)"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,35,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,36,0)="7.  Print message 'Please wait.  Loading all pages ...' in the command"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,37,0)="area right after the user issues a 'Save' command.  If there are a lot"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,38,0)="pages on the form, the loading process could take a while before the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,39,0)="'Verifying ...' message appears.  (DDS41)"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,40,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,41,0)="8.  If we deleted an entry pointed to by a .01 field, we ended up with a"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,42,0)="dangling pointer.  This patch makes ScreenMan display the internal form of"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,43,0)="the dangling .01 field pointer, so that the user can subsequently delete"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,44,0)="that .01, if it's on the form.  (DDS7)"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,45,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,46,0)="9.  In the Form Editor, if you're editing the properties of a field on the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,47,0)="form, at the Caption prompt, you should be able to enter the following"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,48,0)="shortcuts: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,49,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,50,0)="       Shortcut        To define the caption as "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,51,0)="       --------        ------------------------"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,52,0)="       !L              The Label of the field "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,53,0)="       !T              The Title of the field "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,54,0)="       !U              The Unique name of the field "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,55,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,56,0)="Prior to this patch, these shortcuts weren't working.  (DDSCAP)"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,57,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,58,0)="10.  If a repeating block is used to display records in a multiple, and an"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,59,0)="Index is specified for the repeating block, FileMan loads only those"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,60,0)="entries that are in the index.  Prior to this patch, a hard error would"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,61,0)="result if the user selected an entry that was in the multiple but wasn't"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,62,0)="in the index, because ScreenMan hadn't loaded the data for that entry."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,63,0)="This patch allows the user to select only those entries that have already "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,64,0)="been loaded by ScreenMan.  (DDSM)"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,65,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,66,0)="11.  Suppose you have a field with executable that calls EN^DDIOL.  It"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,67,0)="passes a global to EN^DDIOL with formatting nodes so that the text appears"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,68,0)="in two columns.  For example:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,69,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,70,0)="       ^TMP($J,1)=string 1 "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,71,0)="       ^TMP($J,2)=string 1a "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,72,0)="       ^TMP($J,2,""F"")=""?40"" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,73,0)="       ^TMP($J,3)=string 2 "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,74,0)="       ^TMP($J,4)=string 2a "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,75,0)="       ^TMP($J,4,""F"")=""?40"" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,76,0)="       "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,77,0)="        etc.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,78,0)="        "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,79,0)="       D EN^DDIOL("""",""^TMP($J)"") "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,80,0)="        "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,81,0)="When you enter a ? at the field, ScreenMan displays in the help area the "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,82,0)="first 14 lines correctly, but the fifteenth line is incorrect.  The third "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,83,0)="screen looks like this: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,84,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,85,0)=" -------------------------------------------------------------------------"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,86,0)=" string 11                             string 11a "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,87,0)=" string 12                             string 12a "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,88,0)=" string 13                             string 13a "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,89,0)=" string 14                             string 14a "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,90,0)=" string 15 "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,91,0)=" Enter RETURN to continue or '^' to exit: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,92,0)=" -------------------------------------------------------------------------"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,93,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,94,0)="and the fourth screen looks like this: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,95,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,96,0)=" -------------------------------------------------------------------------"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,97,0)="                                       string 15a "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,98,0)=" string 16                             string 16a "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,99,0)=" string 17                             string 17a "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,100,0)=" string 18                             string 18a "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,101,0)=" string 19                             string 19a "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,102,0)=" Enter RETURN to continue or '^' to exit: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,103,0)=" -------------------------------------------------------------------------"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,104,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,105,0)="String 15a should have printed next to string 15, but instead printed on"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,106,0)="the subsequent screen on a line by itself.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,107,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,108,0)="This change fixes this problem.  (DDSMSG)"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,109,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,110,0)="12.  This patch fixes and enhances the POINTER LINK property of blocks."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,111,0)="Suppose you want to display, in a repeating block, entries in a file"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,112,0)="reached via a backward pointer and then from one of those entries,"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,113,0)="you want to follow a forward pointer and branch to a page to display"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,114,0)="entries from the pointed-to file.  No POINTER LINK syntax would allow you"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,115,0)="to define this relationship.  The reference point for the POINTER LINK"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,116,0)="needed to be the Primary File of the form, and the syntax didn't allow for"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,117,0)="navigation via a backward pointer."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,118,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,119,0)="This patch introduces the syntax: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,120,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,121,0)="       DD(file#,pfield#)  or [DD(file#,pfield#1):pfield#2...] "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,122,0)="   or  DD(file#,field#) "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,123,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,124,0)="to the POINTER LINK, where file# is the point of reference to start the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,125,0)="navigation, pfield# is the pointer field that points to the file defined"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,126,0)="by the block's DD#, and field# is the field used as a lookup value in the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,127,0)="file defined by the blocks DD#.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,128,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,129,0)="This patch also allows for navigation via a variable pointer:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,130,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,131,0)="       vpfield#:file# "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,132,0)="   or  vpfield#:file#:pfield:pfield#...  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,133,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,134,0)="where vpfield# is a the number of a variable pointer field.  The key "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,135,0)="difference is that you can specify the file# to which to navigate."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,136,0)="(DDSPTR)"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,137,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,138,0)="13.  If DDSBR was set to ""fieldId^blockId^pageId"", and blockId was the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,139,0)="Block Name rather than the Block Order number, ScreenMan disregarded the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,140,0)="blockId and automatically assumed the first block on the page.  (DDS2)"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,141,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,142,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,143,0)="Routine Summary: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,144,0)="==============="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,145,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,146,0)="The following routines are included in this patch.  The second line of"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,147,0)="these routines now look like:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,148,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,149,0)="   <tab>;;21.0;VA FileMan;**[patch list]**;Dec 28, 1994 "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,150,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,151,0)="CHECK^XTSUMBLD results: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,152,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,153,0)="   Routine Name   Before Patch    After Patch     Patch List "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,154,0)="   ============   ============    ===========     =========="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,155,0)="   DDS0           3530371         3556700         4,11,20"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,156,0)="   DDS01          6353532         6466228         4,13,14,20"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,157,0)="   DDS02          3319070         3361580         20"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,158,0)="   DDS1           4939831         4949444         4,13,20"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,159,0)="   DDS2           8724593         7821612         20"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,160,0)="   DDS41          6131012         6283323         4,20"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,161,0)="   DDS7           4229219         3559395         13,20"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,162,0)="   DDSCAP         862095          860878          13,20"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,163,0)="   DDSM           4593795         4923688         6,13,14,20"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,164,0)="   DDSMSG         2481467         2834198         20"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,165,0)="   DDSPTR         5051244         5419548         20"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,166,0)="   DDSR           7814012         7813886         14,20"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,167,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,168,0)="Installation Instructions: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,169,0)="========================="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,170,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,171,0)="    1.  AXP Sites -  The routines DDS0, DDS01, DDS1, and DDSR are"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,172,0)="usually mapped on systems that allow mapping, so you will need to disable"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,173,0)="mapping for these routines."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,174,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,175,0)="    2.  Use the 'INSTALL/CHECK MESSAGE' option on the PackMan menu. This"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,176,0)="option will load the KIDS package onto your system.  The environment"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,177,0)="check routine, DIENV, will run automatically."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,178,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,179,0)="    DIENV is sent to prevent the inadvertent queuing of the installation"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,180,0)="of this patch."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,181,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,182,0)="    3.  The patch has now been loaded into a transport global on your"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,183,0)="system. You now need to use KIDS to install the transport global."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,184,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,185,0)="    4.  On the KIDS menu, under the 'Installation' menu, use the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,186,0)="following options:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,187,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,188,0)="           Print Transport Global"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,189,0)="           Compare Transport Global to Current System"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,190,0)="           Verify Checksums in Transport Global"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,191,0)="           Backup a Transport Global"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,192,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,193,0)="    5.  Inhibit users from login into the system.  The consequences of"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,194,0)="installing this patch while users are on the system are that users editing"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,195,0)="data via ScreenMan forms will get a 'source routine edited' error, and"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,196,0)="their edits will be lost, and the data may be left in an inconsistent"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,197,0)="state.  Note that PAID is an application that uses ScreenMan."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,198,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,199,0)="    6.  AXP Sites - Place Taskman in a WAIT State."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,200,0)="        MSM Sites - STOP Taskman."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,201,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,202,0)="    7.  On the KIDS menu, under the 'Installation' menu, use the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,203,0)="following option:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,204,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,205,0)="              Select Installation Option: Install Package(s)"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,206,0)="              Select INSTALL NAME: DI*21.0*20"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,207,0)="                                   =========="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,208,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,209,0)="              Answer 'NO' to 'Want to DISABLE Scheduled Options, Menu"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,210,0)="Options, and Protocols?"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,211,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,212,0)="    8.  MSM Sites - Answer YES to the question 'Want to MOVE routines to"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,213,0)="other CPUs?'."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,214,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,215,0)="     Then enter the names of your other Compute and Print server(s)."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,216,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,217,0)="     NOTE: You will then see the following message:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,218,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,219,0)="     TASKMAN is not running. You must run the routine XPDCPU in the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,220,0)="production UCI for each of the VOLUME SETS you have listed once the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,221,0)="installation starts!!"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,222,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,223,0)="    9.  MSM Sites - After the installation begins, you will be prompted"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,224,0)="to enter a DEVICE.  After the device has been entered, KIDS will pause."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,225,0)="At this time, you need to go to each of the CPUs that were entered in"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,226,0)="step 8, and start a job that will allow them to receive the patched"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,227,0)="routines.  On each one, you do the following:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,228,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,229,0)="    a) >D ^XUP    This will set up the programming environment."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,230,0)="        ======"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,231,0)="    b) >D ^XPDCPU This starts the job that receives the patched routines."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,232,0)="        ========="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,233,0)="    c) Select INSTALL NAME: DI*21.0*20"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,234,0)="                            =========="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,235,0)="The installation will then continue."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,236,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,237,0)="    10.  AXP Sites - After the patch has been installed and IF you"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,238,0)="disabled your map set (step 1 above), rebuild your map set."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,239,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,240,0)="    11.  AXP Sites - Remove Taskman from WAIT State."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,241,0)="         MSM Sites - Restart Taskman."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,242,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",8,1,243,0)="    12.  Enable users' login."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,0)="36 SEQ #27^2961227^1"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,0)="^^102^102^2961227"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,1,0)="This patch fixes a bug in ^DIWP that started after patch DI*21*33: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,2,0)="                  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,3,0)="Applications doing the following are affected by this bug::"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,4,0)="              "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,5,0)="       1.  Application calls ^DIWP with DIWL=0."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,6,0)="                                       "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,7,0)="       2.  After the call to ^DIWP, application resets DIWL=0 and then"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,8,0)="accesses the ^UTILITY global.  No text is found when referencing"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,9,0)="^UTILITY($J,""W"",DIWL."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,10,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,11,0)="The related NOIS Calls are: WPB-1296-31219 and NOP-1296-11296."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,12,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,13,0)="This patch reverses the fix sent by patch 33."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,14,0)="                                                                           "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,15,0)=" Routine Summary: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,16,0)=" ================"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,17,0)="  The following routines are included in this patch.  The second line of"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,18,0)="these routines now look like:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,19,0)="                               "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,20,0)="  <tab>;;21.0;VA FileMan;**"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,21,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,22,0)=" Routine Summary: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,23,0)=" ================"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,24,0)="  The following routines are included in this patch.  The second line of"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,25,0)="these routines now look like:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,26,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,27,0)="  <tab>;;21.0;VA FileMan;**[patch list]**;Dec 28, 1994 "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,28,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,29,0)="  CHECK^XTSUMBLD results: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,30,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,31,0)="  Routine Name   Before Patch    After Patch     Patch List "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,32,0)="  ============   ============    ===========     =========="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,33,0)="  DIWP           5150036         5103576         33,36"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,34,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,35,0)=" Installation Instructions: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,36,0)=" =========================="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,37,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,38,0)="    1.  The routines DIW* are usually mapped on systems that allow"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,39,0)="mapping.  Alpha sites that use routine mapping will need to disable"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,40,0)="mapping for these routines."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,41,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,42,0)="    2.  Use the 'INSTALL/CHECK MESSAGE' option on the PackMan menu. This"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,43,0)="option will load the KIDS package onto your system.  The environment check"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,44,0)="routine DIENV will run automatically."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,45,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,46,0)="    The Environment Check routine, DIENV, is sent in order to prevent the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,47,0)="inadvertent queueing of the installation of this patch."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,48,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,49,0)="    3.  The patch has now been loaded into a transport global on your"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,50,0)="system. You now need to use KIDS to install the transport global."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,51,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,52,0)="    4.  On the KIDS menu, under the 'Installation' menu, use the following"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,53,0)="options: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,54,0)="           Print Transport Global "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,55,0)="           Compare Transport Global to Current System "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,56,0)="           Verify Checksums in Transport Global "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,57,0)="           Backup a Transport Global "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,58,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,59,0)="    5.  Inhibit users from login into the system.  If users stay on the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,60,0)="system while installing this patch, a hard error may occur while word"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,61,0)="processing fields are printed or edited."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,62,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,63,0)="    6.  AXP Sites - Place Taskman in a WAIT State.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,64,0)="        MSM Sites - STOP Taskman.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,65,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,66,0)="    7.  On the KIDS menu, under the 'Installation' menu, use the following"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,67,0)="option: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,68,0)="           Select Installation Option:  Install Package(s)"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,69,0)="           Select INSTALL NAME: DI*21.0*36"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,70,0)="                                =========="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,71,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,72,0)="           Answer 'NO' to 'Want to DISABLE Scheduled Options, Menu"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,73,0)="Options, and Protocols?"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,74,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,75,0)="    8.  MSM Sites - Answer YES to the question 'Want to MOVE routines to"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,76,0)="other CPUs?'."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,77,0)="    Then enter the names of your other Compute and Print server(s).  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,78,0)="    NOTE: You will then see the following message: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,79,0)="       TASKMAN is not running. You must run the routine XPDCPU in the "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,80,0)="production UCI for each of the VOLUME SETS you have listed once the "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,81,0)="installation starts!! "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,82,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,83,0)="    9.  MSM Sites - After the installation begins, you will be prompted to"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,84,0)="enter a DEVICE.  After the device has been entered, KIDS will pause.  At"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,85,0)="this time, you need to go to each of the CPUs that were entered in step 8,"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,86,0)="and start a job that will allow them to receive the patched routines.  On"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,87,0)="each one, you do the following:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,88,0)="    a) >D ^XUP    This will set up the programming environment.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,89,0)="        ======"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,90,0)="    b) >D ^XPDCPU  This starts the job that receives the patched routines."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,91,0)="        ========="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,92,0)="    c) Select INSTALL NAME: DI*21.0*36"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,93,0)="                            =========="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,94,0)="The installation will then continue.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,95,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,96,0)="    10.  AXP Sites - After the patch has been installed, rebuild your map"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,97,0)="set. "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,98,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,99,0)="    11.  AXP Sites - Remove Taskman from WAIT State.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,100,0)="         MSM Sites - Restart Taskman.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,101,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",9,1,102,0)="    12.  Enable users' login.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,0)="24 SEQ #28^2970206^1"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,0)="^^323^323^2970206"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,1,0)="This patch makes the following changes: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,2,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,3,0)="1.  Given the following: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,4,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,5,0)="    a. You have a file with a pointer-type .01 field called NAME."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,6,0)="    b. NAME points to a file that has a free text field called SHORT"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,7,0)="       DESCRIPTION.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,8,0)="    c. NAME has NAME:SHORT DESCRIPTION as an output transform.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,9,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,10,0)="    If you Enter/Edit the entries in this file and enter ^LOOP at the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,11,0)="'Select NAME:' prompt you can get a hard error."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,12,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,13,0)="    NOIS: KAN-0396-41540"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,14,0)="    (DIA2)"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,15,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,16,0)="2.  For set of codes ^DIR Reader calls, adds a new input array: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,17,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,18,0)="    DIR(""L"",#) Only applies to set of codes.  Replaces the standard "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,19,0)="               vertical listing of codes that the Reader displays.  It is "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,20,0)="               up to the programmer to ensure that the contents of the "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,21,0)="               DIR(""L"") array matches the codes in the second ^-piece of "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,22,0)="               DIR(0).  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,23,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,24,0)="               The format of the DIR(""L"") array is similar to DIR(""A"") "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,25,0)="               and DIR(""?"").  The #'s must be numeric starting from 1. The"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,26,0)="               numbered lines are written first and the DIR(""L"") is "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,27,0)="               written last."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,28,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,29,0)="    Example:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,30,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,31,0)="    >S DIR(0)=""SO^1:ONE;2:TWO;3:THREE;4:FOUR;5:FIVE;6:SIX;7:SEVEN"""
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,32,0)="    >S DIR(""L"",1)=""Select one of the following:"""
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,33,0)="    >S DIR(""L"",2)="""""
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,34,0)="    >S DIR(""L"",3)=""  1  ONE      5  FIVE"""
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,35,0)="    >S DIR(""L"",4)=""  2  TWO      6  SIX"""
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,36,0)="    >S DIR(""L"",5)=""  3  THREE    7  SEVEN"""
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,37,0)="    >S DIR(""L"")=""  4  FOUR"""
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,38,0)="    >D ^DIR"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,39,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,40,0)="    Select one of the following:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,41,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,42,0)="    1  ONE      5  FIVE"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,43,0)="    2  TWO      6  SIX"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,44,0)="    3  THREE    7  SEVEN"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,45,0)="    4  FOUR"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,46,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,47,0)="    Enter response: ?"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,48,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,49,0)="    Enter a code from the list."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,50,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,51,0)="    Select one of the following:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,52,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,53,0)="    1  ONE      5  FIVE"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,54,0)="    2  TWO      6  SIX"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,55,0)="    3  THREE    7  SEVEN"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,56,0)="    4  FOUR"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,57,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,58,0)="    Enter response: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,59,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,60,0)="Also, for set of codes ^DIR Reader calls, this patch removes unnecessary"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,61,0)="blank lines that appeared before and after the vertical list of codes."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,62,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,63,0)="Also, when using DIR(""?"") - one line help - this patch makes sure a period"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,64,0)="(.) terminates DIR(""?"")."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,65,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,66,0)="Also, this patch makes sure that DIR(""?"") is not overwritten by the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,67,0)="Reader's default help prompt."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,68,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,69,0)="(DIR)"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,70,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,71,0)="3.  Two problems with the FIELD^DID are corrected with this patch:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,72,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,73,0)="    a.  If the FIELD^DID call was used to retrieve field level attributes"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,74,0)="of a file with a file number less than or equal to 1, error code 200 would"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,75,0)="be returned: ""An input variable or parameter is missing or invalid"".  This"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,76,0)="patch makes FIELD^DID work for fields in files numbered 1 or less."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,77,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,78,0)="    b.  FIELD^DID returned erroneous values for FIELD LENGTH for fields"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,79,0)="with data types of MUMPS, SET OF CODES, VARIABLE POINTER, and DATE/TIME."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,80,0)="This patch makes FIELD^DID return more accurate values for FIELD LENGTH"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,81,0)="for these types of fields."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,82,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,83,0)="    (DID, DIQGDD, DIQGDDF, DIQGDDU)"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,84,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,85,0)="4.  This patch corrects the problem which can be reproduced by doing the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,86,0)="following:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,87,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,88,0)="    (1) On the FileMan menu, select OTHER OPTIONS -> AUDITING"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,89,0)="    (2) Select Audit Option #1, FIELDS BEING AUDITED."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,90,0)="    (2) Pick a file that has no fields being audited."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,91,0)="    (3) Select Audit Option #2, DATA DICTIONARIES BEING AUDTED."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,92,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,93,0)="    An undefined variable DFF error would occur."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,94,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,95,0)="    NOIS: ISB-1096-30117"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,96,0)="    (DIAU)"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,97,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,98,0)="5.  If on a field you tried to put an output transform that looked like"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,99,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,100,0)="    S:'$D(var) var=...     or"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,101,0)="    D:'...                 or"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,102,0)="    X:'..."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,103,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,104,0)="and in the file, there was a field with a name that started with the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,105,0)="letter before the colon (:), an infinite loop would occur.  This patch"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,106,0)="corrects this problem.  (DICOMPX)"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,107,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,108,0)="6.  If the cross references on a file are compiled, and the file contains"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,109,0)="trigger-type cross references, FileMan could break the routine within a do"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,110,0)="block structure, so that the first line of the next routine starts with a"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,111,0)=".line.  For example, the last few lines of one routine could be:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,112,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,113,0)="         I X'="""" D"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,114,0)="         .N DIK,DIV,DIU,DIN"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,115,0)="  END    G ^zzzz2"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,116,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,117,0)="and the first few lines of the next routine could be:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,118,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,119,0)="  zzzz2  ; COMPILED XREF FOR FILE #xxxxx ; 10/30/96"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,120,0)="         ; "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,121,0)="         .K DIV S DIV=X,D0=DA,DIV(0)=D0 S Y(1)=$S($D(^DIZ(xxxxx,D0,3)):"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,122,0)="           ^(3),1:"""")S X=$P(Y(1),U,2),X=X S DIU=X K Y X ^DD(xxxxx,2,1,"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,123,0)="           12,2.1) X ^DD(xxxxx,2,1,12,2.4)"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,124,0)="         ..."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,125,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,126,0)="This patch ensure that routines are not broken within do block structures."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,127,0)="(DIKZ)"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,128,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,129,0)="7.  This patch also fixes several problems with %DT and time input:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,130,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,131,0)="    a. If only numbers are entered in the date and/or time position, like"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,132,0)="       9999999999999999999999999999999, an illegal number error could"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,133,0)="       result."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,134,0)="       "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,135,0)="    b. Even with %DT[""N"", %DT was accepting noncanonic numbers (numbers"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,136,0)="       with a leading 0).  So, 090196 was accepted, but 100196 was not."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,137,0)="       "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,138,0)="    c. If only a time is input, and the AM/PM designation on the time"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,139,0)="       contained punctuation (like 2:00 A.M.), the input was rejected."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,140,0)="       "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,141,0)="    d. A single 0 in the minutes or seconds position of the time, could"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,142,0)="       cause the time to be interpreted incorrectly.  For example, 01:0"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,143,0)="       was interpreted as 10:00 am, and T@01:01:0 was interpreted as"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,144,0)="       10:00:10 am.  With this fix, time with a single 0 in the minutes or"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,145,0)="       seconds position is rejected."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,146,0)="       "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,147,0)="    e. Time with hours of 0 or 00 were not getting interpreted"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,148,0)="       correctly.  For example:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,149,0)="                                            Intepreted as"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,150,0)="                                            ============="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,151,0)="                       Input             Before fix  After fix"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,152,0)="                       =====             ==========  ========="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,153,0)="         %DT'[""S""      [0]0:01:03     => 01:03       rejected"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,154,0)="                       [0]0:01:03 AM  => 01:03       rejected"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,155,0)="                       [0]0:01:03 PM  => 13:03       rejected"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,156,0)="         "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,157,0)="                          0:01        => 13:00       00:01"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,158,0)="                       [0]0:01 AM     => 01:00       00:01"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,159,0)="                       [0]0:01 PM     => 13:00       rejected"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,160,0)="         "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,161,0)="         %DT[""S""          0:01:03     => 13:00:03    00:01:03"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,162,0)="                       [0]0:01:03 AM  => 01:00:03    00:01:03"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,163,0)="                       [0]0:01:03 PM  => 13:00:03    rejected"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,164,0)="         "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,165,0)="                          0:01        => 13:00:00    00:01:00"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,166,0)="                       [0]0:01 AM     => 01:00:00    00:01:00"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,167,0)="                       [0]0:01 PM     => 13:00:00    rejected"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,168,0)="       "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,169,0)="    f. T@14AM translated to 00:14.  After this fix, T@14AM is rejected."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,170,0)="       "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,171,0)="    g. If %DT[""S"" and the time contained seconds, and there was at least"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,172,0)="       one space between the time and the AM/PM designator, the time was"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,173,0)="       rejected.  For example, T@1:00:00 PM was rejected, T@1:00:00PM was"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,174,0)="       accepted. (This bug fix applies to DSM, not MSM, sites.)"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,175,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,176,0)="    h. If only a year and a time is entered, %DT was accepting the input"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,177,0)="       and echoing back a time without hours or minutes:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,178,0)="        "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,179,0)="       >S %DT=""AEST"""
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,180,0)="       >D ^%DT"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,181,0)="        "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,182,0)="       DATE: 1995@9  (1995@::00)"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,183,0)="        "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,184,0)="       >ZW Y"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,185,0)="       Y=2950000.09"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,186,0)="        "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,187,0)="       This change makes %DT reject this input."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,188,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,189,0)="    NOIS: MAR-0996-21548"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,190,0)="    (DIDT)"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,191,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,192,0)="8.  If the user enters something like P at the THEN PRINT FIELDS prompt, and"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,193,0)="there are no fields that begin with P, DICOMP was looking at the ""PT"""
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,194,0)="nodes to find all backpointing files that begin with P.  This could lead"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,195,0)="to confusing messages like 'such and such pointer is not cross"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,196,0)="referenced', or 'by ... do you mean... '."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,197,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,198,0)="    NOIS: ISH-1194-40032"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,199,0)="    (DICOMP0)"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,200,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,201,0)="9.  If a line in a word processing field sent to ^DIWP contains a vertical"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,202,0)="bar, followed by alphanumerics, followed by punctuation, followed by more"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,203,0)="alphanumerics, an undefined J(0) error can occur at DD^DICOMPV, if there"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,204,0)="is at least one file on the system whose name starts with the characters"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,205,0)="between the vertical bar and the punctuation. For example, the following"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,206,0)="code produces the error:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,207,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,208,0)="    S X=""A line of text <vertical bar>A:B"""
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,209,0)="    S DIWF=""N"",DIWL=10,DIWR=68"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,210,0)="    D ^DIWP"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,211,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,212,0)="if there are any files on the system that start with 'A'."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,213,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,214,0)="    NOIS: BRX-1196-11544"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,215,0)="          DES-1096-41943"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,216,0)="          DES-1296-40431"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,217,0)="          HAM-1096-20548"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,218,0)="          HIN-1196-41175"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,219,0)="          KNX-1196-40451"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,220,0)="    (DICOMP0)"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,221,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,222,0)="Routine Summary: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,223,0)="==============="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,224,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,225,0)="  Routine Name    Before Patch    After Patch    Patch List "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,226,0)="  ============    ============    ===========    =========="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,227,0)="  DIA2            4082017         4038834        24"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,228,0)="  DIAU            6095280         6112601        24"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,229,0)="  DICOMP0         9824026         9896481        24"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,230,0)="  DICOMPX         3752529         3767023        24"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,231,0)="  DID             9150099         9151079        24"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,232,0)="  DIDT            6692350         6401681        18,24"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,233,0)="  DIKZ            9722237         9897922        6,24"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,234,0)="  DIQGDD          7426471         6585703        24"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,235,0)="  DIQGDDF         N/A             1999058        24"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,236,0)="  DIQGDDU         1298733         1578963        24"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,237,0)="  DIR             8215233         8414467        13,24"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,238,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,239,0)="Installation Instructions: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,240,0)="========================="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,241,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,242,0)="    1.  AXP Sites - The routines %DT and DIR are usually mapped on"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,243,0)="systems that allow mapping, so you will need to disable mapping for these"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,244,0)="routines."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,245,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,246,0)="    2.  Use the 'INSTALL/CHECK MESSAGE' option on the PackMan menu. This"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,247,0)="option will load the KIDS package onto your system.  The environment"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,248,0)="check routine, DIENV, will run automatically."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,249,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,250,0)="    DIENV is sent to prevent the inadvertent queuing of the installation"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,251,0)="of this patch."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,252,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,253,0)="    3.  The patch has now been loaded into a transport global on your"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,254,0)="system. You now need to use KIDS to install the transport global."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,255,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,256,0)="    4.  On the KIDS menu, under the 'Installation' menu, use the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,257,0)="following options:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,258,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,259,0)="           Print Transport Global"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,260,0)="           Compare Transport Global to Current System"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,261,0)="           Verify Checksums in Transport Global"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,262,0)="           Backup a Transport Global"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,263,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,264,0)="    5.  Inhibit users from login into the system.  Some of the routines in"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,265,0)="this patch are used heavily in DHCP applications, most notably %DT and"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,266,0)="DIR. The consequences of installing this patch while users are on the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,267,0)="system are that users may get a 'source routine edited' error.  Due to the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,268,0)="high usage of these routines in all of DHCP code, it is highly recommended"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,269,0)="that users be off the system when installing this patch."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,270,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,271,0)="    6.  AXP Sites - Place Taskman in a WAIT State."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,272,0)="        MSM Sites - STOP Taskman."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,273,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,274,0)="    7.  On the KIDS menu, under the 'Installation' menu, use the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,275,0)="following option:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,276,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,277,0)="              Select Installation Option: Install Package(s)"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,278,0)="              Select INSTALL NAME: DI*21.0*24"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,279,0)="                                   =========="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,280,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,281,0)="              Answer 'NO' to 'Want to DISABLE Scheduled Options, Menu"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,282,0)="Options, and Protocols?"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,283,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,284,0)="    8.  MSM Sites - Answer YES to the question 'Want to MOVE routines to"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,285,0)="other CPUs?'."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,286,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,287,0)="        Then enter the names of your other Compute and Print server(s)."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,288,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,289,0)="        NOTE: You will then see the following message:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,290,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,291,0)="        TASKMAN is not running. You must run the routine XPDCPU in the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,292,0)="production UCI for each of the VOLUME SETS you have listed once the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,293,0)="installation starts!!"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,294,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,295,0)="    9.  MSM Sites - After the installation begins, you will be prompted"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,296,0)="to enter a DEVICE.  After the device has been entered, KIDS will pause."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,297,0)="At this time, you need to go to each of the CPUs that were entered in"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,298,0)="step 8, and start a job that will allow them to receive the patched"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,299,0)="routines.  On each one, you do the following:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,300,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,301,0)="    a) >D ^XUP    This will set up the programming environment."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,302,0)="        ======"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,303,0)="    b) >D ^XPDCPU This starts the job that receives the patched routines."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,304,0)="        ========="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,305,0)="    c) Select INSTALL NAME: DI*21.0*24"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,306,0)="                            =========="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,307,0)="The installation will then continue."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,308,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,309,0)="    10.  After installing this patch, you need to move routine DIDT"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,310,0)="from your production account to the manager account.  Then, in the manager"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,311,0)="account, rename:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,312,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,313,0)="         DIDT to %DT"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,314,0)="      "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,315,0)="         Example: ZL DIDT ZS %DT"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,316,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,317,0)="    11.  AXP Sites - After the patch has been installed, rebuild your map"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,318,0)="set."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,319,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,320,0)="    12.  AXP Sites - Remove Taskman from WAIT State."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,321,0)="         MSM Sites - Restart Taskman."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,322,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",10,1,323,0)="    13.  Enable users' login."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,0)="15 SEQ #18^2970303^1"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,0)="^^104^104^2970303"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,1,0)="This patch fixes 4 problems: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,2,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,3,0)="1.  In a KIDS build entry, one can send data with a file.  The field 'Data"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,4,0)="List' may contain the name of a Search or Inquire Template.  If several"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,5,0)="files in a BUILD entry use the same template name the DIFROM Server"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,6,0)="correctly processes only the first file's template.  The subsequent"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,7,0)="files' templates are processed incorrectly and it could abort the building"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,8,0)="of a transport global. (DIFROMSU)"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,9,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,10,0)="2.  When the DIFROM Server installs Data Dictionaries, it's possible that"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,11,0)="the 'I' specifier on the file's data global 0 node could be deleted.  This"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,12,0)="prevents 'Identifiers' from getting displayed.  This was reported on NOIS"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,13,0)="STC-0596-40915.  (DIFROMSS) "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,14,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,15,0)="3.  When a word processing window can't be resolved by FileMan during any"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,16,0)="FM output, a hard error occurs.  This patch fixes this problem.  This was"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,17,0)="reported on NOIS SFC-0395-60476. (DIWW)"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,18,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,19,0)="4.  When FileMan tries to resolve a word processing window with multiple "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,20,0)="arguments via a call to ^DIWP, a hard error occurs.  (DICOMP) "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,21,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,22,0)=" Routine Summary: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,23,0)=" ================"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,24,0)="  The following routines are included in this patch.  The second line of"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,25,0)="these routines now look like:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,26,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,27,0)="  <tab>;;21.0;VA FileMan;**[patch list]**;Dec 28, 1994 "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,28,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,29,0)="  CHECK^XTSUMBLD results: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,30,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,31,0)="  Routine Name   Before Patch    After Patch     Patch List "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,32,0)="  ============   ============    ===========     =========="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,33,0)="  DICOMP         5808899         5821767         15 "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,34,0)="  DIFROMSS       3496020         3490849         15 "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,35,0)="  DIFROMSU       5182472         5222720         10,15 "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,36,0)="  DIWW           5725412         5618941         15 "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,37,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,38,0)=" Installation Instructions: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,39,0)=" =========================="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,40,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,41,0)="    1.  The routine DIWW is usually mapped on systems that allow mapping,"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,42,0)="so you will need to disable mapping for this routine."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,43,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,44,0)="    2.  Use the 'INSTALL/CHECK MESSAGE' option on the PackMan menu. This"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,45,0)="option will load the KIDS package onto your system.  Say 'No' to 'Want to"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,46,0)="RUN the Environment Check routine?."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,47,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,48,0)="    The Environment Check routine, DIENV, is sent in order to prevent the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,49,0)="inadvertent queueing of the installation of this patch."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,50,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,51,0)="    3.  The patch has now been loaded into a transport global on your"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,52,0)="system. You now need to use KIDS to install the transport global."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,53,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,54,0)="    4.  On the KIDS menu, under the 'Installation' menu, use the following"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,55,0)="options: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,56,0)="           Print Transport Global "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,57,0)="           Compare Transport Global to Current System "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,58,0)="           Verify Checksums in Transport Global "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,59,0)="           Backup a Transport Global "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,60,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,61,0)="    5.  Inhibit users from login into the system.  If users stay on the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,62,0)="system while installing this patch, a hard error may occur while word"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,63,0)="processing fields are printed."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,64,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,65,0)="    6.  AXP Sites - Place Taskman in a WAIT State.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,66,0)="        MSM Sites - STOP Taskman.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,67,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,68,0)="    7.  On the KIDS menu, under the 'Installation' menu, use the following"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,69,0)="option: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,70,0)="           Install Package(s): "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,71,0)="           INSTALL NAME: DI*21.0*15 "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,72,0)="                         =========="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,73,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,74,0)="           Answer 'NO' to 'Want to DISABLE Scheduled Options, Menu"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,75,0)="Options, and Protocols?"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,76,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,77,0)="    8.  MSM Sites - Answer YES to the question 'Want to MOVE routines to"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,78,0)="other CPUs?'."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,79,0)="    Then enter the names of your other Compute and Print server(s).  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,80,0)="    NOTE: You will then see the following message: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,81,0)="       TASKMAN is not running. You must run the routine XPDCPU in the "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,82,0)="production UCI for each of the VOLUME SETS you have listed once the "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,83,0)="installation starts!! "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,84,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,85,0)="    9.  MSM Sites - After the installation begins, you will be prompted to"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,86,0)="enter a DEVICE.  After the device has been entered, KIDS will pause.  At"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,87,0)="this time, you need to go to each of the CPUs that were entered in step 8,"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,88,0)="and start a job that will allow them to receive the patched routines.  On"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,89,0)="each one, you do the following:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,90,0)="    a) >D ^XUP    This will set up the programming environment.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,91,0)="        ======"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,92,0)="    b) >D ^XPDCPU  This starts the job that receives the patched routines."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,93,0)="        ========="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,94,0)="    c) Select INSTALL NAME: DI*21.0*15 "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,95,0)="                            =========="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,96,0)="The installation will then continue.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,97,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,98,0)="    10.  AXP Sites - After the patch has been installed, rebuild your map"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,99,0)="set. "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,100,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,101,0)="    11.  AXP Sites - Remove Taskman from WAIT State.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,102,0)="         MSM Sites - Restart Taskman.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,103,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",11,1,104,0)="    12.  Enable user's login.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,0)="29 SEQ #30^2970306^1"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,0)="^^315^315^2970306"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,1,0)="Prerequisites:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,2,0)="=============="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,3,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,4,0)="Progress Notes patch GMRP*2.5*46 should be installed at the same time as"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,5,0)="this FileMan patch.  Below is a brief description of that patch.  If the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,6,0)="Progress Notes patch is not installed, it will result only in a minor"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,7,0)="annoyance as described, but will not affect any data."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,8,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,9,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,10,0)="If FileMan patch DI*21*29 is installed without installing patch"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,11,0)="GMRP*2.5*46, and if the site has the 'ASK TITLE OK?' parameter in file"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,12,0)="121.99 (field 3.5) turned on and the user does the lookup on the title"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,13,0)="file by Internal Entry Number (IEN) the '...OK? YES//' prompt will be"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,14,0)="displayed twice."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,15,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,16,0)="After GMRP*2.5*46 has been installed, the user will only be prompted once"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,17,0)="under those conditions."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,18,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,19,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,20,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,21,0)="Patch Description"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,22,0)="================="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,23,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,24,0)="This patch fixes many bugs and makes some minor enhancements in the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,25,0)="FileMan lookup (^DIC, IX^DIC and MIX^DIC1) entry points."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,26,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,27,0)="The patch addresses the following NOIS and E3R:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,28,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,29,0)="NOIS:                          E3R:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,30,0)="========                       ======="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,31,0)="ANN-0496-42241                 7556"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,32,0)="BIR93-2-2097"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,33,0)="CLE-1295-41595"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,34,0)="CLL94-3-0077"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,35,0)="DAY-0895-42009                  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,36,0)="DUB-0696-30598                  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,37,0)="HUN-0896-21930"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,38,0)="ISL-0794-50399"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,39,0)="KNX-0396-41289"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,40,0)="LEA-0496-41204"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,41,0)="LON-0296-62113"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,42,0)="MAD-0694-40057"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,43,0)="MWV-1295-21154"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,44,0)="NOL-0896-71128"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,45,0)="OKL-0396-70731"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,46,0)="PAL-1096-60467"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,47,0)="REN93-4-0762"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,48,0)="SFC-0696-61382"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,49,0)="TUC-0295-60054"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,50,0)="WCO-0195-50090"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,51,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,52,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,53,0)="In addition, the patch fixes the following bugs/shortcomings reported in"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,54,0)="mail messages and not through the NOIS or E3R system.:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,55,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,56,0)="ONLINE HELP/PROMPTS:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,57,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,58,0)="  a) Question mark help on a set of codes wasn't right aligning the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,59,0)="external value of the possible choices in the display.  Reported by Cathy"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,60,0)="Williamson (Hines IRMFO)."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,61,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,62,0)="  b) If adding a new entry failed due to failure during execution of a"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,63,0)="LAYGO node on the file, or because the file is restricted from editing"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,64,0)="(such as an archive file), FileMan quit without giving any indication. Now"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,65,0)="it will question mark beep so user knows something went wrong.  Reported"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,66,0)="locally by Don Creaven."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,67,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,68,0)="  c) When doing a lookup on a multiple field with a whole-file"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,69,0)="cross-reference (like a SYNONYM multiple), the entry from the x-ref was"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,70,0)="being displayed, but not the .01 at the top level of the selected entry."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,71,0)="Reported by Robert Melmoth (Hines IFMRO)."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,72,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,73,0)="  d) When doing a lookup on some index other than the .01, only the value"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,74,0)="from the index is displayed, and not the value of the .01 field itself."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,75,0)="Reported by Tom Ash (Montgomery)"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,76,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,77,0)="  e) A new field SHORT DESCRIPTION was added to the DIALOG file.  A WRITE"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,78,0)="identifier was added to display this field on lookup.  Suggested by Mike"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,79,0)="Kilmade."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,80,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,81,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,82,0)="HARD ERRORS:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,83,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,84,0)="  a) Bad variable name error at NAME+1^DICM2, when displaying DIC(""B"")"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,85,0)="default value when following a pointer to a variable pointer.  Reported by"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,86,0)="Mary Ann Ferrarese in Record Tracking."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,87,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,88,0)="  b) NOPEN error when job is queued with no device, DIC(0)[""Q"" and lookup"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,89,0)="fails.  It tried to write #7,??.  Reported by Skip Ormsby (Clarksburg"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,90,0)="VAMC)."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,91,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,92,0)="  c) UNDEF error at R+1^DIC2 when a cross-reference entry exists but no"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,93,0)="corresponding entry exists in the data.  Found during testing of FileMan"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,94,0)="21 at Oklahoma City VAMC."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,95,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,96,0)="  d) UNDEF error in ALL^DICM2 was caused when we had a file with the .01"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,97,0)="field a variable pointer, the .01 field of the first pointed-to file is a"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,98,0)="pointer, user selects the value on the pointed-to file, presses return"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,99,0)="when matches on the home file are selected, then FileMan starts to look at"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,100,0)="the second file pointed-to by the variable pointer.  Found by Tom"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,101,0)="Ackerman."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,102,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,103,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,104,0)="LOOKUPS USING POINTER FIELDS:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,105,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,106,0)="  a) Fixed a lookup problem in Social Work Case File (650) reported by"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,107,0)="Kevin McGee, San Francisco VAMC. Lookup with lower case letter + last 4"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,108,0)="digits SSN was very slow, and could potentially fail to find a valid match"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,109,0)="as well."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,110,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,111,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,112,0)="LOOKUPS WHERE DIC(0)[""N"":"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,113,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,114,0)="  a) When FileMan does a lookup by IEN because DIC(0)[""N"" and it can find"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,115,0)="no other matches, make it ask OK?...  Reported by Clayton Curtis as a"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,116,0)="MailMan bug when he accidently entered a number at the recipient prompt"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,117,0)="(in an attempt to edit a line number of the message.)."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,118,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,119,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,120,0)="VARIABLE PRESERVATION,CLEANUP,OUTPUT:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,121,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,122,0)="  a) Users lookup value X wasn't being protected while executing"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,123,0)="IDENTIFIER logic.  Reported by George Huggins, Indian Health Service."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,124,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,125,0)="  b) When user was entering a new entry with DIC(0)'[""A"", and up-arrowed"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,126,0)="out either during the entry of a required IDENTIFIER, or at the 'Are you"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,127,0)="Adding...' message, FileMan wasn't setting output variable DUOUT. Reported"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,128,0)="by Robert Whelan, Albany IRMFO."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,129,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,130,0)="  c) UTILITY option to EDIT a file wasn't cleaning up the DR variable at"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,131,0)="the end.  If the PRINT option was run immediately afterwards, it tried to"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,132,0)="use DR as an input variable with unexpected results.  Reported by Skip"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,133,0)="Ormsby, Clarksburg VAMC."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,134,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,135,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,136,0)="MIX^DIC1 AND IX^DIC CALLS:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,137,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,138,0)="  a) Honor cross-reference list D in an MIX^DIC1 call, when FileMan goes"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,139,0)="off to look on cross-references for pointer, date, set and variable"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,140,0)="pointer fields on the home file, transforms lookup value from lower to"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,141,0)="upper case, and other types of transforms.  Previously FileMan was looking"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,142,0)="at all x-refs during this part of the logic.  Reported by Greg Kreis."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,143,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,144,0)="  b) Using the MIX^DIC1 call, if one of the x-refs in input variable ""D"""
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,145,0)="is on a pointer field, we do a lookup in the pointed-to file.  FileMan was"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,146,0)="still using the list of indexes in ""D"" on the pointed-to file, even though"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,147,0)="they only had meaning in the original home file. Reported by Tom Love"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,148,0)="(Indian Health Service)."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,149,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,150,0)="  c) If developer did an MIX^DIC1 or IX^DIC call with D containing a"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,151,0)="cross-reference before ""B"" (ex. ""AC""), and lookup value was a pointer,"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,152,0)="set, variable pointer or date, then lookup could only find the entry by"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,153,0)="its internal value.  Reported by Greg Kreis."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,154,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,155,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,156,0)="PROBLEMS WITH PARTIAL MATCHING:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,157,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,158,0)="  a) Problem stuffing the .01 field of a multiple with ""///"" in an input"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,159,0)="template, when field is defined as a date with time. When user enters a"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,160,0)="date with time as a lookup value, FileMan converts it to its FileMan"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,161,0)="internal value, then if it finds a partial match to that date/time,"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,162,0)="considers that to be a match.  If there is just one partial match, this"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,163,0)="entry is selected.  This was a problem when user was trying to stuff a"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,164,0)="date/time entry into a multiple with the 3 slash syntax.  The fix is for"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,165,0)="FileMan to automatically put an X into DIC(0), thus forcing exact matches"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,166,0)="only, when the 3 slash syntax is used in an input template.  Found by"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,167,0)="Cindy Heuer (Hines IRMFO) in Radiology."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,168,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,169,0)="  b) FileMan did not properly find partial matches to a numeric lookup"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,170,0)="value, if the value in the index was alpha-numeric, but started with a"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,171,0)="number containing decimals.  This was found at Tucson VAMC by Jim Orrey."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,172,0)="They had a file where entries contained rational numbers, but they had"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,173,0)="created an index with a trailing blank, in order to force string"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,174,0)="collation."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,175,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,176,0)="  c) If .01 field is longer than 30 characters, user enters a lookup value"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,177,0)="greater than 30 characters in lower or mixed case, FileMan failed to find"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,178,0)="valid matches to the lookup value.  Reported by Dave Bolduc (AIMC"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,179,0)="programmer)"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,180,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,181,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,182,0)="PROBLEMS ADDING NEW RECORDS:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,183,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,184,0)="  a) If a process held onto a LOCK on what would have been the next record"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,185,0)="to be added to a file before incrementing the next record number count in "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,186,0)="the header node, any other process trying to add an entry to that file"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,187,0)="would get stuck in an infinite loop (unless/until the first process"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,188,0)="released its lock)."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,189,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,190,0)="  b) If, contrary to the instruction on page 65 of the FileMan version 21 "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,191,0)="Programmers Reference Manual, a routine called FILE^DICN with the local "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,192,0)="variable DD set equal to ""B"", or the name of some other index in the data "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,193,0)="file, or the record number of an existing record, or 0, that routine would"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,194,0)="get stuck in an infinite loop."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,195,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,196,0)="  c) If a routine called FILE^DICN with DD set to some other value, the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,197,0)="new record would be added to the file with that as its record number, even"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,198,0)="if DD's value was not a legal record number. This erroneous behavior even"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,199,0)="overrode the use of DINUM."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,200,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,201,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,202,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,203,0)="In addition, a new flag DIC(0)[""B"" was added.  The purpose of the flag is"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,204,0)="to restrict FileMan to looking only on the ""B"" index of any files"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,205,0)="pointed-to by the file in which you're doing the lookup.  The complete API"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,206,0)="description is available in HTML format at:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,207,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,208,0)="   http:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,209,0)=" //www.vista.domain.ext/softserv/infrastr.uct/fileman/docs/pm/cl-dic-f.htm"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,210,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,211,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,212,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,213,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,214,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,215,0)=" Routine Summary: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,216,0)=" ================"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,217,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,218,0)=" The following routines are included in this patch.  The second line of"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,219,0)="these routines now look like:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,220,0)="   "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,221,0)="   <tab>;;21.0;VA FileMan;**[patch list]**;Dec 28, 1994 "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,222,0)="   "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,223,0)=" CHECK^XTSUMBLD results: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,224,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,225,0)="   Routine Name   Before Patch    After Patch     Patch List "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,226,0)="   ============   ============    ===========     =========="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,227,0)="DIA                 6481291         7724491               29"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,228,0)="DIC                 9887383        10229217             8,29"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,229,0)="DIC1                7157061         7440729               29"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,230,0)="DIC2                3386307         3335934               29"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,231,0)="DICM                8204058         9718946               29"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,232,0)="DICM0               4918663         4859257             6,29"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,233,0)="DICM2               6920486         7132763               29"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,234,0)="DICM3               4620259         5089665               29"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,235,0)="DICN                7476631         7973339         12,19,29"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,236,0)="DICN1               7148963         5825723               29"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,237,0)="DICQ                8535882         9280954               29"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,238,0)="DICQ1               5481016         5602061               29"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,239,0)="DIE1                6179725         6188430               29"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,240,0)="DIE17               6896286         6919106            19,29"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,241,0)="DIE3                4863655         4850886               29"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,242,0)="DIOU                4914520         5001668             8,29"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,243,0)="DIU                 4034034         4050319               29"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,244,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,245,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,246,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,247,0)=" Installation Instructions: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,248,0)=" =========================="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,249,0)="   "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,250,0)="     1.  The routines DIC, DIC1, DIC2, DICM, DICM0, DICM2, DICM3, DICN,"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,251,0)="DICN1, DICQ, DICQ1, DIE1, DIE17 and DIOU are usually mapped on systems"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,252,0)="that allow mapping, AXP sites will need to disable mapping for these"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,253,0)="routines."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,254,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,255,0)="     2.  Use the 'INSTALL/CHECK MESSAGE' option on the PackMan menu. This"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,256,0)="option will load the KIDS package onto your system.  (The environment"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,257,0)="check routine DIENV will run automatically.  DIENV is sent in order to"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,258,0)="prevent the inadvertent queueing of the installation of this patch.)"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,259,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,260,0)="     3.  The patch has now been loaded into a transport global on your"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,261,0)="system. You now need to use KIDS to install the transport global."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,262,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,263,0)="     4.  On the KIDS menu, under the 'Installation' menu, use the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,264,0)="following options:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,265,0)="            Print Transport Global "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,266,0)="            Compare Transport Global to Current System "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,267,0)="            Verify Checksums in Transport Global "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,268,0)="            Backup a Transport Global "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,269,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,270,0)="     5.  Inhibit users from login into the system.  The consequence of"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,271,0)="having users on the system while installing this patch are that users"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,272,0)="adding or editing records will get a 'source routine edited' error, the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,273,0)="edits will be lost and records may be left in an inconsistent state.  Note"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,274,0)="that most of these routines are heavily used in DHCP applications."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,275,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,276,0)="     6.  AXP Sites - Place Taskman in a WAIT State.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,277,0)="         MSM Sites - STOP Taskman.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,278,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,279,0)="     7.  On the KIDS menu, under the 'Installation' menu, use the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,280,0)="following option:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,281,0)="            Select Installation Option: Install Package(s) "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,282,0)="            Select INSTALL NAME: DI*21.0*29 "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,283,0)="                                 =========="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,284,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,285,0)="            Answer 'NO' to 'Want to DISABLE Scheduled Options, Menu"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,286,0)="Options, and Protocols?"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,287,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,288,0)="     8.  MSM Sites - Answer YES to the question 'Want to MOVE routines to"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,289,0)="other CPUs?'. Then enter the names of your other Compute and Print"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,290,0)="server(s).  NOTE: You will then see the following message:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,291,0)="   TASKMAN is not running. You must run the routine XPDCPU in the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,292,0)="production UCI for each of the VOLUME SETS you have listed once the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,293,0)="installation starts!!"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,294,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,295,0)="     9.  MSM Sites - After the installation begins, you will be prompted"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,296,0)="to enter a DEVICE.  After the device has been entered, KIDS will pause. At"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,297,0)="this time, you need to go to each of the CPUs that were entered in step 8,"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,298,0)="and start a job that will allow them to receive the patched routines.  On"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,299,0)="each one, you do the following:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,300,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,301,0)="     a) >D ^XUP    This will set up the programming environment.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,302,0)="         ======"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,303,0)="     b) >D ^XPDCPU  This starts the job that receives the patched routines"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,304,0)="         ========="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,305,0)="     c) Select INSTALL NAME: DI*21.0*29"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,306,0)="                             =========="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,307,0)="The installation will then continue."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,308,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,309,0)="     10.  AXP Sites - After the patch has been installed, rebuild your map"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,310,0)="set. "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,311,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,312,0)="     11.  AXP Sites - Remove Taskman from WAIT State.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,313,0)="          MSM Sites - Restart Taskman.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,314,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",12,1,315,0)="     12.  Enable user's login.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,0)="40 SEQ #31^2970414^1"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,0)="^^95^95^2970414"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,1,0)="  Patch Description: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,2,0)="  ============"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,3,0)="  This patch fixes a rare bug.  An UNDEF error can occur if user does a"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,4,0)="queued print of a JOIN EXTENDED POINTER computed field in a file.  Patch"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,5,0)="DI*21*29 killed Y in ^DIC, before resetting Y to the documented output"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,6,0)="value. This happens only when ^DIC was called from a queued job.  In most"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,7,0)="cases this causes no problems, however, subscripted values of the variable"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,8,0)="Y are used in the computed field logic of JOIN EXTENDED POINTER type"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,9,0)="computed fields, causing an UNDEF error when these fields are printed in a"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,10,0)="queued print job. This patch corrects the problem."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,11,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,12,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,13,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,14,0)="  Routine Summary: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,15,0)="  ================"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,16,0)="   "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,17,0)="  The following routines are included in this patch.  The second line of"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,18,0)="these routines now look like:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,19,0)="    "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,20,0)="  <tab>;;21.0;VA FileMan;**[patch list]**;Dec 28, 1994"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,21,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,22,0)="  CHECK^XTSUMBLD results: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,23,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,24,0)="    Routine Name   Before Patch    After Patch     Patch List "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,25,0)="    ============   ============    ===========     =========="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,26,0)="    DIC                10229217       10080729        8,29,40 "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,27,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,28,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,29,0)="   "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,30,0)="  Installation Instructions: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,31,0)="  =========================="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,32,0)="    "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,33,0)="      1.  The routine DIC is usually mapped on systems that allow mapping,"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,34,0)="AXP sites will need to disable mapping for this routine."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,35,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,36,0)="      2.  Use the 'INSTALL/CHECK MESSAGE' option on the PackMan menu. This"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,37,0)=" option will load the KIDS package onto your system.  (The environment"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,38,0)="check routine DIENV will run automatically.  DIENV is sent in order to"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,39,0)="prevent the inadvertent queueing of the installation of this patch.)"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,40,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,41,0)="      3.  The patch has now been loaded into a transport global on your"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,42,0)="system. You now need to use KIDS to install the transport global."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,43,0)="   "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,44,0)="      4.  On the KIDS menu, under the 'Installation' menu, use the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,45,0)="following options:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,46,0)="   Print Transport Global "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,47,0)="   Compare Transport Global to Current System "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,48,0)="   Verify Checksums in Transport Global "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,49,0)="   Backup a Transport Global "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,50,0)="   "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,51,0)="      5.  Inhibit users from login into the system.  The consequence of"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,52,0)="having users on the system while installing this patch are that users"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,53,0)="adding or editing records will get a 'source routine edited' error, the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,54,0)="edits will be lost and records may be left in an inconsistent state.  Note"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,55,0)="that this routine is heavily used in DHCP applications."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,56,0)="   "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,57,0)="      6.  AXP Sites - Place Taskman in a WAIT State.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,58,0)="          MSM Sites - STOP Taskman.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,59,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,60,0)="      7.  On the KIDS menu, under the 'Installation' menu, use the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,61,0)="following option:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,62,0)=" Select Installation Option: Install Package(s)"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,63,0)="             Select INSTALL NAME: DI*21.0*40 "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,64,0)="                                  =========="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,65,0)="   "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,66,0)="             Answer 'NO' to 'Want to DISABLE Scheduled Options, Menu"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,67,0)="Options, and Protocols?"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,68,0)="   "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,69,0)="      8.  MSM Sites - Answer YES to the question 'Want to MOVE routines to"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,70,0)="other CPUs?'. Then enter the names of your other Compute and Print"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,71,0)="server(s). NOTE: You will then see the following message: TASKMAN is not"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,72,0)="running. You must run the routine XPDCPU in the production UCI for each of"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,73,0)="the VOLUME SETS you have listed once the installation starts!!"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,74,0)="   "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,75,0)="      9.  MSM Sites - After the installation begins, you will be prompted"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,76,0)="to enter a DEVICE.  After the device has been entered, KIDS will pause. At"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,77,0)="this time, you need to go to each of the CPUs that were entered in step 8,"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,78,0)="and start a job that will allow them to receive the patched routine.  On"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,79,0)="each one, you do the following:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,80,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,81,0)="    a) >D ^XUP    This will set up the programming environment.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,82,0)="        ======"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,83,0)="    b) >D ^XPDCPU  This starts the job that receives the patched routines."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,84,0)="        ========="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,85,0)="    c) Select INSTALL NAME: DI*21.0*40 "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,86,0)="                            =========="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,87,0)=" The installation will then continue.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,88,0)="   "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,89,0)="      10.  AXP Sites - After the patch has been installed, rebuild your"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,90,0)="map set."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,91,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,92,0)="      11.  AXP Sites - Remove Taskman from WAIT State.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,93,0)="           MSM Sites - Restart Taskman.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,94,0)="   "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",13,1,95,0)="      12.  Enable user's login.  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,0)="42 SEQ #32^2970819^1"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,0)="^^214^214^2970819"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,1,0)=" Description:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,2,0)=" ============"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,3,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,4,0)="      This patch changes two different features of FileMan in important"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,5,0)="      ways. The changes are to 1) how the Finder DBS call processes"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,6,0)="      commas in lookup values, and 2) the order in which cross-"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,7,0)="      references fire during reindexing. Although these changes are NOT"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,8,0)="      backward-compatible, they have not disrupted sites we've tested"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,9,0)="      at, and provide a great deal of benefit in return."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,10,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,11,0)="      For a complete explanation, please read this description"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,12,0)="      carefully. Because these changes are not backward-compatible, we"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,13,0)="      have taken time to explain the reasons and effects in detail. For"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,14,0)="      more information on these changes, send email to"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,15,0)="      G.FMTEAM@DOMAIN.EXT, or call Rick Marshall at 206-764-2283."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,16,0)="      Please notify us immediately if you experience any ill effects"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,17,0)="      from either of these changes."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,18,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,19,0)="      Change 1: Finder and Comma-Piecing"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,20,0)="      ----------------------------------"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,21,0)="      The Finder, like the classic DIC Lookup call, processes lookup"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,22,0)="      values that contain commas specially. We have reduced the power of "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,23,0)="      the Finder's algorithm in order to buy a substantial performance "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,24,0)="      gain, and have introduced a new ""C"" flag to allow callers to get "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,25,0)="      the full power of the original algorithm when they need it. DIC's "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,26,0)="      algorithm has not been changed, which means DIC and the Finder do "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,27,0)="      not handle commas the same way unless you include the ""C"" flag in "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,28,0)="      your Finder call."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,29,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,30,0)="      The need to make this backward-incompatible change arises from "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,31,0)="      another difference between the classic DIC lookup and the Finder: "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,32,0)="      short-circuiting. In general, the Finder call was designed to "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,33,0)="      emulate the DIC lookup, but one feature of DIC caused a great deal "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,34,0)="      of confusion: the way DIC short-circuits its lookup whenever it "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,35,0)="      has possible matches. DIC rarely presents all of the matches "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,36,0)="      available, but instead at fixed points during the lookup will quit "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,37,0)="      early if it has any matches. This feature is designed to focus the "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,38,0)="      lookup on what DIC considers the most likely matches, but many "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,39,0)="      users report they find the behavior counter-intuitive."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,40,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,41,0)="      For this reason, the Finder was designed to always locate all "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,42,0)="      possible matches, to avoid short-circuiting out of the lookup "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,43,0)="      early. Since the Finder always runs all relevant parts of the "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,44,0)="      lookup algorithm, it always pays the performance costs of those "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,45,0)="      parts. Comma-piecing, a powerful but expensive part of DIC's "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,46,0)="      lookup, was usually short-circuited out of DIC's main lookup, but "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,47,0)="      in the Finder, of course, is always run on lookup values "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,48,0)="      containing commas. Thus, with values containing commas, the Finder "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,49,0)="      is usually much slower than DIC."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,50,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,51,0)="      The Imaging project, in particular, discovered that the "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,52,0)="      performance cost was too much to pay, and asked the FileMan team "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,53,0)="      to change the Finder's behavior. Although comma-piecing is "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,54,0)="      documented (FileMan 21 Programmer Manual, pages 208-209, and "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,55,0)="      FileMan 21 User Manual, page 12 (""^ST,ADD"" in part 3)), most users "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,56,0)="      polled do not realize the full power of this feature, but believe "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,57,0)="      it has a limited capability specific to names. Although the "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,58,0)="      FileMan team rarely considers making backward-incompatible "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,59,0)="      changes, in this case most experts were unaware of and therefore "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,60,0)="      not relying on the parts of this algorithm that made it expensive. "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,61,0)="      In return for making this change, lookups at Houston VAMC "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,62,0)="      involving values containing commas passed to the Finder that used "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,63,0)="      to take 90 seconds now take 4. The sheer magnitude of the trade-"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,64,0)="      off led us to reduce the power of comma-piecing Finder lookups to "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,65,0)="      what people have known about, while adding the ""C"" flag for those "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,66,0)="      who know about and need the full power."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,67,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,68,0)="      Most users and developers believe comma piecing is specifically "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,69,0)="      used with names, so that a lookup values like ""DI,MI"" will match "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,70,0)="      ""DISTASO,MICHAEL"" and ""DISNEY,MICHELLE"". In other words, the comma "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,71,0)="      in the lookup value stands for a real comma in the index value, "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,72,0)="      and a partial match is run on both the first and last names. This "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,73,0)="      is what the Finder has been changed to do."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,74,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,75,0)="      What comma-piecing does in DIC, what it did in the Finder before "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,76,0)="      this patch, and what the Finder does after this patch if the ""C"" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,77,0)="      flag is passed is far more powerful. The comma stands for any "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,78,0)="      arbitrary string of characters followed by any punctuation "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,79,0)="      character. ""DI,MI"" then will match not just the values most users "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,80,0)="      expect, but also ""DISTASO,XACHARY MICHAEL"" and ""DINNER WITH "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,81,0)="      MICHAEL"". This is the principle behind the ""^ST,ADD"" example in "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,82,0)="      the FileMan 21 User Manual. This feature requires a tremendous "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,83,0)="      amount of processing, but yields far more flexible results, and "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,84,0)="      programmers are encouraged to use the Finder's new ""C"" flag when "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,85,0)="      appropriate."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,86,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,87,0)="      Change 2: Reindexing Fire Order Change"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,88,0)="      --------------------------------------"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,89,0)="      The order in which cross-references fire can be crucial. We have "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,90,0)="      changed this order to correct what we perceive to be a flaw in the "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,91,0)="      algorithm. By its nature, this can affect the integrity of the "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,92,0)="      database. We believe the new order is the correct one, but if "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,93,0)="      there is code in VistA that relies on the old order this change "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,94,0)="      has the capability of introducing database corruption. We did not "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,95,0)="      make this change lightly. Not only do we believe the new fire "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,96,0)="      order is correct, but it is likely that code that relied upon the "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,97,0)="      old fire order was already introducing database corruption. We "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,98,0)="      have found no evidence yet of code that relied on the old fire "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,99,0)="      order, but in a system this complex we can never be sure."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,100,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,101,0)="      Most cross-reference are not sensitive to the order in which they "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,102,0)="      are fired. For example, a regular cross-reference simply creates "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,103,0)="      an index entry based on the current field value, and has no effect "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,104,0)="      on other field values or other indexes. However, a trigger does "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,105,0)="      affect other fields, and will affect other indexes if those fields "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,106,0)="      are cross-referenced. For example, if an entire record is being "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,107,0)="      reindexed, a cross-reference on the .01 field might trigger a new "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,108,0)="      value into field 1. If field 1 is cross-referenced before field "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,109,0)="      .01, field 1's indexes will use the old value instead of the new "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,110,0)="      one that the .01 trigger will put there."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,111,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,112,0)="      When you create a new entry, you enter the values for the .01 "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,113,0)="      field before any other fields (generally speaking), so the cross-"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,114,0)="      references on the .01 field fire first. However, before this "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,115,0)="      patch, when reindexing a record FileMan always saves the cross-"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,116,0)="      references on the .01 field for last. Using our previous example "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,117,0)="      of the trigger on the .01, this means when adding an entry the "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,118,0)="      cross-references on field 1 use the new value triggered by the .01 "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,119,0)="      but when reindexing field 1's cross-references fire first, using "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,120,0)="      the old value, and then cross-references on field .01 fire, "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,121,0)="      triggering the new value into field 1 but not affecting its cross-"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,122,0)="      references."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,123,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,124,0)="      This effectively prevents you from writing code sensitive to fire "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,125,0)="      order that will work when reindexed. After a great deal of "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,126,0)="      consideration, we have decided that the fire order for the Set "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,127,0)="      Logic should always fire as if a new entry were being added; that "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,128,0)="      is, the .01 cross-references always fire first even when "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,129,0)="      reindexing. We decide the Kill Logic is firing in the correct "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,130,0)="      order already; that is, the kill logic for the .01 should fire "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,131,0)="      last so that FileMan-compatibility is preserved by keeping a B "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,132,0)="      cross-reference on any record for as long as possible."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,133,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,134,0)="      The Merge package uses extensive reindexing when merging two "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,135,0)="      records together, and requested this change. This change made the "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,136,0)="      package easier to code, and has produced no visible side effects "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,137,0)="      at the test sites."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,138,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,139,0)=" Test Sites :"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,140,0)=" ============"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,141,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,142,0)="      Finder and Comma-Piecing Change:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,143,0)="      --------------------------------"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,144,0)="      Houston VAMC"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,145,0)="      The Imaging Project Team"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,146,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,147,0)="      Reindexing Fire Order Change:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,148,0)="      -----------------------------"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,149,0)="      Columbia VAMC"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,150,0)="      Tucson VAMC"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,151,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,152,0)=" Routine Summary :"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,153,0)=" ================="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,154,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,155,0)="      The following is a list of the routines included in this patch."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,156,0)="      The second line of each of these routines now looks like:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,157,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,158,0)="      <tab>;;21.0;VA FileMan;**patch list**;Dec 28, 1994"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,159,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,160,0)=" Checksums According to CHECK^XTSUMBLD:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,161,0)=" ======================================"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,162,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,163,0)="   Rtn Nm   Chksum Before   Chksum After  Patch List"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,164,0)="   ------   -------------   ------------  ----------"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,165,0)="   DICF     7072808         7066780       17,27,42"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,166,0)="   DICF1    5555141         5908138       42"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,167,0)="   DICF3    6296720         9189474       17,42"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,168,0)="   DIK      7325945         7352303       42"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,169,0)="   DIK1     5820262         5943602       42"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,170,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,171,0)="      To test if the transport has been corrupted, you can use the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,172,0)="      ""Verify Package Integrity"" option.  This option is under Utilities"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,173,0)="      under the KIDS menu.  It will compare the checksums in the build"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,174,0)="      with the current checksums and list any that differ."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,175,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,176,0)=" Installation Instructions:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,177,0)=" =========================="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,178,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,179,0)=" NOTE any instructions that are different for 486 or Alpha sites."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,180,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,181,0)="      1.   Users ARE NOT allowed to be on the system during the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,182,0)="      installation."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,183,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,184,0)="      2.   DO place TaskMan in a WAIT state."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,185,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,186,0)="      3.   These routines are not usually mapped on systems that allow"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,187,0)="      it so you will probably not have to disable mapping."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,188,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,189,0)="      4.   Use the 'INSTALL/CHECK MESSAGE' option on the PackMan menu."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,190,0)="      This option will load the KIDS package onto your system."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,191,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,192,0)="      5.   The patch has now been loaded into a Transport global on your"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,193,0)="      system. You now need to use KIDS to install the Transport global."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,194,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,195,0)="      6.   On the KIDS menu, under the 'Installation' menu, use the"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,196,0)="      following options:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,197,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,198,0)="            Verify Checksums in Transport Global"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,199,0)="            Print Transport Global"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,200,0)="            Compare Transport Global to Current System"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,201,0)="            Backup a Transport Global"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,202,0)="            Install Package(s)"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,203,0)="            INSTALL NAME: DI*21.0*42"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,204,0)="                          =========="
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,205,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,206,0)="      7.   MSM Sites - Answer YES to the question 'Want to MOVE"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,207,0)="      routines to other CPUs?'. Enter the names of your Compute and"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,208,0)="      Print server(s)."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,209,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,210,0)="           AXP Sites - Answer NO to this question."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,211,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,212,0)="      8.   Rebuild your mapped set if necessary."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,213,0)="  "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",14,1,214,0)="      9.   DO remove TaskMan from a WAIT state."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",15,0)="28 SEQ #33^2970819^1"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",15,1,0)="^^71^71^2970819"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",15,1,1,0)="Description:"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",15,1,2,0)="============"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",15,1,3,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",15,1,4,0)="     This patch changes FileMan's DIM (MUMPS Code Validation) entry point"
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",15,1,5,0)="     to validate against the 1995 ANSI Standard and the new VA SAC."
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",15,1,6,0)=" "
^DIC(9.4,13,22,7,"PAH",15,1,7,0)="Test Sites :"
